| C085 | 4.18.1→4.18.3 | パッチ | 高 | 破壊的変更(要確認) | camel-google-functions, camel-google-secret-manager | camel-google-functions / camel-google-secret-manager - potential breaking change | 原文全文を読み、該当するオプション/クラス/ヘッダー/URI を grep。使用していれば修正が必要 | camel-google-functions, camel-google-secret-manager, GoogleCloudFunctionsConstants, GoogleSecretManagerConstants, GoogleCloudFunctions, GoogleSecretManager, Camel, HeaderFilterStrategy, GoogleCloudFunctionsConstants.OPERATION, GoogleCloudFunctionsOperation, CamelGoogleCloudFunctionsOperation, GoogleCloudFunctionsConstants.ENTRY_POINT, GoogleCloudFunctionsEntryPoint, CamelGoogleCloudFunctionsEntryPoint | The Exchange header constants in `GoogleCloudFunctionsConstants` and `GoogleSecretManagerConstants` that carried a `GoogleCloudFunctions` / `GoogleSecretManager` prefix are not in the `Camel` namespace and were therefore not filtered by the default `HeaderFilterStrategy`. They have been renamed to add the `Camel` prefix. The Java field names are unchanged; only the header string values have changed: \| Constant \| Previous value \| New value \| `GoogleCloudFunctionsConstants.OPERATION` \| `GoogleClou |  | The Exchange header constants in `GoogleCloudFunctionsConstants` and<br>`GoogleSecretManagerConstants` that carried a `GoogleCloudFunctions` /<br>`GoogleSecretManager` prefix are not in the `Camel` namespace and were<br>therefore not filtered by the default `HeaderFilterStrategy`. They have been<br>renamed to add the `Camel` prefix. The Java field names are unchanged; only<br>the header string values have changed:<br><br>[options="header"]<br>\|===<br>\| Constant \| Previous value \| New value<br>\| `GoogleCloudFunctionsConstants.OPERATION` \| `GoogleCloudFunctionsOperation` \| `CamelGoogleCloudFunctionsOperation`<br>\| `GoogleCloudFunctionsConstants.ENTRY_POINT` \| `GoogleCloudFunctionsEntryPoint` \| `CamelGoogleCloudFunctionsEntryPoint`<br>\| `GoogleCloudFunctionsConstants.RUNTIME` \| `GoogleCloudFunctionsRuntime` \| `CamelGoogleCloudFunctionsRuntime`<br>\| `GoogleCloudFunctionsConstants.SOURCE_ARCHIVE_URL` \| `GoogleCloudFunctionsSourceArchiveUrl` \| `CamelGoogleCloudFunctionsSourceArchiveUrl`<br>\| `GoogleCloudFunctionsConstants.RESPONSE_OBJECT` \| `GoogleCloudFunctionsResponseObject` \| `CamelGoogleCloudFunctionsResponseObject`<br>\| `GoogleSecretManagerConstants.OPERATION` \| `GoogleSecretManagerOperation` \| `CamelGoogleSecretManagerOperation`<br>\|===<br><br>The `GoogleSecretManagerConstants.SECRET_ID`, `VERSION_ID` and `REPLICATION`<br>constants were already `Camel`-prefixed and are unchanged.<br><br>Routes that reference the constants symbolically (for example<br>`setHeader(GoogleCloudFunctionsConstants.OPERATION, ...)`) continue to work<br>without changes. Routes that set the header by its literal string value (for<br>example `setHeader("GoogleCloudFunctionsOperation", ...)`) must be updated to<br>use the new value (`setHeader("CamelGoogleCloudFunctionsOperation", ...)`).<br><br>The generated Endpoint DSL header accessor names are unchanged (for example<br>`googleCloudFunctionsOperation()`), since the `Camel` prefix is stripped when<br>deriving the accessor name; the accessors now return the new `Camel`-prefixed<br>values.<br><br>NOTE: The companion rename for `camel-google-vision`, `camel-google-text-to-speech`<br>and `camel-google-speech-to-text` from the same main-branch PR (#23467) is<br>**not** backported to 4.18.x because those components were added after the<br>4.18.x branch point and do not exist on this maintenance branch. | 未調査 |  |
| C086 | 4.18.1→4.18.3 | パッチ | 高 | 破壊的変更(要確認) | camel-openstack | camel-openstack - potential breaking change | 原文全文を読み、該当するオプション/クラス/ヘッダー/URI を grep。使用していれば修正が必要 | camel-openstack, OpenstackConstants, KeystoneConstants, NovaConstants, CinderConstants, GlanceConstants, NeutronConstants, SwiftConstants, OpenstackConstants.OPERATION, operation, CamelOpenstackOperation, OpenstackConstants.ID, ID, CamelOpenstackId | The Exchange header constants in `OpenstackConstants`, `KeystoneConstants`, `NovaConstants`, `CinderConstants`, `GlanceConstants`, `NeutronConstants`, and `SwiftConstants` have been renamed to follow the Camel naming convention used across the rest of the component catalog. The Java field names are unchanged; only the header string values have changed. Common constants (in `OpenstackConstants`): \| Constant \| Previous value \| New value \| `OpenstackConstants.OPERATION` \| `operation` \| `CamelOpenst | あり | The Exchange header constants in `OpenstackConstants`, `KeystoneConstants`,<br>`NovaConstants`, `CinderConstants`, `GlanceConstants`, `NeutronConstants`,<br>and `SwiftConstants` have been renamed to follow the Camel naming convention<br>used across the rest of the component catalog. The Java field names are<br>unchanged; only the header string values have changed.<br><br>Common constants (in `OpenstackConstants`):<br><br>[options="header"]<br>\|===<br>\| Constant \| Previous value \| New value<br>\| `OpenstackConstants.OPERATION` \| `operation` \| `CamelOpenstackOperation`<br>\| `OpenstackConstants.ID` \| `ID` \| `CamelOpenstackId`<br>\| `OpenstackConstants.NAME` \| `name` \| `CamelOpenstackName`<br>\| `OpenstackConstants.DESCRIPTION` \| `description` \| `CamelOpenstackDescription`<br>\| `OpenstackConstants.PROPERTIES` \| `properties` \| `CamelOpenstackProperties`<br>\|===<br><br>Keystone (`KeystoneConstants`):<br><br>[options="header"]<br>\|===<br>\| Constant \| Previous value \| New value<br>\| `KeystoneConstants.DESCRIPTION` \| `description` \| `CamelOpenstackKeystoneDescription`<br>\| `KeystoneConstants.DOMAIN_ID` \| `domainId` \| `CamelOpenstackKeystoneDomainId`<br>\| `KeystoneConstants.PARENT_ID` \| `parentId` \| `CamelOpenstackKeystoneParentId`<br>\| `KeystoneConstants.PASSWORD` \| `password` \| `CamelOpenstackKeystonePassword`<br>\| `KeystoneConstants.EMAIL` \| `email` \| `CamelOpenstackKeystoneEmail`<br>\| `KeystoneConstants.USER_ID` \| `userId` \| `CamelOpenstackKeystoneUserId`<br>\| `KeystoneConstants.GROUP_ID` \| `groupId` \| `CamelOpenstackKeystoneGroupId`<br>\|===<br><br>Nova (`NovaConstants`):<br><br>[options="header"]<br>\|===<br>\| Constant \| Previous value \| New value<br>\| `NovaConstants.FLAVOR_ID` \| `FlavorId` \| `CamelOpenstackNovaFlavorId`<br>\| `NovaConstants.RAM` \| `RAM` \| `CamelOpenstackNovaRam`<br>\| `NovaConstants.VCPU` \| `VCPU` \| `CamelOpenstackNovaVcpu`<br>\| `NovaConstants.DISK` \| `disk` \| `CamelOpenstackNovaDisk`<br>\| `NovaConstants.SWAP` \| `swap` \| `CamelOpenstackNovaSwap`<br>\| `NovaConstants.RXTXFACTOR` \| `rxtxFactor` \| `CamelOpenstackNovaRxtxFactor`<br>\| `NovaConstants.ADMIN_PASSWORD` \| `AdminPassword` \| `CamelOpenstackNovaAdminPassword`<br>\| `NovaConstants.IMAGE_ID` \| `ImageId` \| `CamelOpenstackNovaImageId`<br>\| `NovaConstants.KEYPAIR_NAME` \| `KeypairName` \| `CamelOpenstackNovaKeypairName`<br>\| `NovaConstants.NETWORK` \| `NetworkId` \| `CamelOpenstackNovaNetworkId`<br>\| `NovaConstants.ACTION` \| `action` \| `CamelOpenstackNovaAction`<br>\|===<br><br>Cinder (`CinderConstants`):<br><br>[options="header"]<br>\|===<br>\| Constant \| Previous value \| New value<br>\| `CinderConstants.SIZE` \| `size` \| `CamelOpenstackCinderSize`<br>\| `CinderConstants.VOLUME_TYPE` \| `volumeType` \| `CamelOpenstackCinderVolumeType`<br>\| `CinderConstants.IMAGE_REF` \| `imageRef` \| `CamelOpenstackCinderImageRef`<br>\| `CinderConstants.SNAPSHOT_ID` \| `snapshotId` \| `CamelOpenstackCinderSnapshotId`<br>\| `CinderConstants.IS_BOOTABLE` \| `isBootable` \| `CamelOpenstackCinderIsBootable`<br>\| `CinderConstants.VOLUME_ID` \| `volumeId` \| `CamelOpenstackCinderVolumeId`<br>\| `CinderConstants.FORCE` \| `force` \| `CamelOpenstackCinderForce`<br>\|===<br><br>Glance (`GlanceConstants`):<br><br>[options="header"]<br>\|===<br>\| Constant \| Previous value \| New value<br>\| `GlanceConstants.DISK_FORMAT` \| `diskFormat` \| `CamelOpenstackGlanceDiskFormat`<br>\| `GlanceConstants.CONTAINER_FORMAT` \| `containerFormat` \| `CamelOpenstackGlanceContainerFormat`<br>\| `GlanceConstants.OWNER` \| `owner` \| `CamelOpenstackGlanceOwner`<br>\| `GlanceConstants.IS_PUBLIC` \| `isPublic` \| `CamelOpenstackGlanceIsPublic`<br>\| `GlanceConstants.MIN_RAM` \| `minRam` \| `CamelOpenstackGlanceMinRam`<br>\| `GlanceConstants.MIN_DISK` \| `minDisk` \| `CamelOpenstackGlanceMinDisk`<br>\| `GlanceConstants.SIZE` \| `size` \| `CamelOpenstackGlanceSize`<br>\| `GlanceConstants.CHECKSUM` \| `checksum` \| `CamelOpenstackGlanceChecksum`<br>\|===<br><br>Neutron (`NeutronConstants`):<br><br>[options="header"]<br>\|===<br>\| Constant \| Previous value \| New value<br>\| `NeutronConstants.TENANT_ID` \| `tenantId` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronTenantId`<br>\| `NeutronConstants.NETWORK_ID` \| `networkId` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronNetworkId`<br>\| `NeutronConstants.ADMIN_STATE_UP` \| `adminStateUp` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronAdminStateUp`<br>\| `NeutronConstants.NETWORK_TYPE` \| `networkType` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronNetworkType`<br>\| `NeutronConstants.PHYSICAL_NETWORK` \| `physicalNetwork` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronPhysicalNetwork`<br>\| `NeutronConstants.SEGMENT_ID` \| `segmentId` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronSegmentId`<br>\| `NeutronConstants.IS_SHARED` \| `isShared` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronIsShared`<br>\| `NeutronConstants.IS_ROUTER_EXTERNAL` \| `isRouterExternal` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronIsRouterExternal`<br>\| `NeutronConstants.ENABLE_DHCP` \| `enableDHCP` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronEnableDhcp`<br>\| `NeutronConstants.GATEWAY` \| `gateway` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronGateway`<br>\| `NeutronConstants.IP_VERSION` \| `ipVersion` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronIpVersion`<br>\| `NeutronConstants.CIDR` \| `cidr` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronCidr`<br>\| `NeutronConstants.SUBNET_POOL` \| `subnetPools` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronSubnetPools`<br>\| `NeutronConstants.DEVICE_ID` \| `deviceId` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronDeviceId`<br>\| `NeutronConstants.MAC_ADDRESS` \| `macAddress` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronMacAddress`<br>\| `NeutronConstants.ROUTER_ID` \| `routerId` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronRouterId`<br>\| `NeutronConstants.SUBNET_ID` \| `subnetId` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronSubnetId`<br>\| `NeutronConstants.PORT_ID` \| `portId` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronPortId`<br>\| `NeutronConstants.ITERFACE_TYPE` \| `interfaceType` \| `CamelOpenstackNeutronInterfaceType`<br>\|===<br><br>Swift (`SwiftConstants`):<br><br>[options="header"]<br>\|===<br>\| Constant \| Previous value \| New value<br>\| `SwiftConstants.CONTAINER_NAME` \| `containerName` \| `CamelOpenstackSwiftContainerName`<br>\| `SwiftConstants.OBJECT_NAME` \| `objectName` \| `CamelOpenstackSwiftObjectName`<br>\| `SwiftConstants.LIMIT` \| `limit` \| `CamelOpenstackSwiftLimit`<br>\| `SwiftConstants.MARKER` \| `marker` \| `CamelOpenstackSwiftMarker`<br>\| `SwiftConstants.END_MARKER` \| `end_marker` \| `CamelOpenstackSwiftEndMarker`<br>\| `SwiftConstants.DELIMITER` \| `delimiter` \| `CamelOpenstackSwiftDelimiter`<br>\| `SwiftConstants.PATH` \| `path` \| `CamelOpenstackSwiftPath`<br>\|===<br><br>`SwiftConstants.CONTAINER_METADATA_PREFIX`, `SwiftConstants.VERSIONS_LOCATION`,<br>`SwiftConstants.CONTAINER_READ`, and `SwiftConstants.CONTAINER_WRITE`<br>intentionally keep their previous values (`X-Container-Meta-`,<br>`X-Versions-Location`, `X-Container-Read`, `X-Container-Write`) because they<br>are part of the Swift HTTP protocol contract used by openstack4j to forward<br>container metadata and ACLs to the Swift backend. Renaming them would break<br>interoperability with the Swift API.<br><br>Routes that reference the constants symbolically (for example<br>`setHeader(OpenstackConstants.OPERATION, ...)`) continue to work without<br>changes. Routes that set the header by its literal string value (for example<br>`setHeader("operation", ...)`) must be updated to use the new value<br>(`setHeader("CamelOpenstackOperation", ...)`).<br><br>The generated Endpoint DSL header accessors on each component's<br>`HeaderNameBuilder` are renamed accordingly (`operation()` -><br>`openstackOperation()`, `password()` -> `openstackKeystonePassword()`,<br>`adminPassword()` -> `openstackNovaAdminPassword()`, etc.). | 未調査 |  |
| C087 | 4.18.1→4.18.3 | パッチ | 高 | 破壊的変更(要確認) | camel-shiro | camel-shiro - potential breaking change | 原文全文を読み、該当するオプション/クラス/ヘッダー/URI を grep。使用していれば修正が必要 | camel-shiro, ShiroSecurityConstants, Camel, SHIRO_SECURITY_TOKEN, SHIRO_SECURITY_USERNAME, SHIRO_SECURITY_PASSWORD, HeaderFilterStrategy, ShiroSecurityConstants.SHIRO_SECURITY_TOKEN, CamelShiroSecurityToken, ShiroSecurityConstants.SHIRO_SECURITY_USERNAME, CamelShiroSecurityUsername, ShiroSecurityConstants.SHIRO_SECURITY_PASSWORD, CamelShiroSecurityPassword, setHeader(ShiroSecurityConstants.SHIRO_SECURITY_USERNAME, ...) | The three Exchange header constants in `ShiroSecurityConstants` that drive Shiro authentication used header values outside the `Camel` namespace (`SHIRO_SECURITY_TOKEN`, `SHIRO_SECURITY_USERNAME`, `SHIRO_SECURITY_PASSWORD`) and were therefore not filtered by the default `HeaderFilterStrategy`. They have been renamed to follow the Camel naming convention. The Java field names are unchanged; only the header string values have changed: \| Constant \| Previous value \| New value \| `ShiroSecurityConstan | あり | The three Exchange header constants in `ShiroSecurityConstants` that drive<br>Shiro authentication used header values outside the `Camel` namespace<br>(`SHIRO_SECURITY_TOKEN`, `SHIRO_SECURITY_USERNAME`, `SHIRO_SECURITY_PASSWORD`)<br>and were therefore not filtered by the default `HeaderFilterStrategy`. They<br>have been renamed to follow the Camel naming convention. The Java field names<br>are unchanged; only the header string values have changed:<br><br>[options="header"]<br>\|===<br>\| Constant \| Previous value \| New value<br>\| `ShiroSecurityConstants.SHIRO_SECURITY_TOKEN` \| `SHIRO_SECURITY_TOKEN` \| `CamelShiroSecurityToken`<br>\| `ShiroSecurityConstants.SHIRO_SECURITY_USERNAME` \| `SHIRO_SECURITY_USERNAME` \| `CamelShiroSecurityUsername`<br>\| `ShiroSecurityConstants.SHIRO_SECURITY_PASSWORD` \| `SHIRO_SECURITY_PASSWORD` \| `CamelShiroSecurityPassword`<br>\|===<br><br>These headers carry credentials and a serialized authentication token, so<br>filtering them at transport boundaries by default is particularly important.<br><br>Routes that reference the constants symbolically (for example<br>`setHeader(ShiroSecurityConstants.SHIRO_SECURITY_USERNAME, ...)`) continue to<br>work without changes. Routes that set the header by its literal string value<br>(for example `setHeader("SHIRO_SECURITY_USERNAME", ...)`) must be updated to<br>use the new value (`setHeader("CamelShiroSecurityUsername", ...)`).<br><br>Because the three header values are now in the `Camel*` namespace, transports<br>that filter Camel-internal headers by default (JMS, CXF, HTTP, etc.) will<br>strip the serialized Shiro authentication token before publishing. This is<br>the intended behavior for untrusted producers. Trusted Shiro-over-transport<br>routes that previously relied on the token surviving the boundary must opt<br>those three headers back in via a custom `HeaderFilterStrategy`, for example:<br><br>[source,java]<br>[code]<br>    public class ShiroFriendlyJmsHeaderFilterStrategy extends JmsHeaderFilterStrategy {<br>        @Override<br>        public boolean applyFilterToCamelHeaders(String name, Object value, Exchange ex) {<br>            if (isShiroSecurityHeader(name)) {<br>                return false;<br>            }<br>            return super.applyFilterToCamelHeaders(name, value, ex);<br>        }<br>    <br>        @Override<br>        public boolean applyFilterToExternalHeaders(String name, Object value, Exchange ex) {<br>            if (isShiroSecurityHeader(name)) {<br>                return false;<br>            }<br>            return super.applyFilterToExternalHeaders(name, value, ex);<br>        }<br>    <br>        private static boolean isShiroSecurityHeader(String name) {<br>            return ShiroSecurityConstants.SHIRO_SECURITY_TOKEN.equalsIgnoreCase(name)<br>                    \|\| ShiroSecurityConstants.SHIRO_SECURITY_USERNAME.equalsIgnoreCase(name)<br>                    \|\| ShiroSecurityConstants.SHIRO_SECURITY_PASSWORD.equalsIgnoreCase(name);<br>        }<br>    }<br>    <br>    jmsComponent.setHeaderFilterStrategy(new ShiroFriendlyJmsHeaderFilterStrategy());<br><br>A worked example is in `ShiroOverJmsTest` in the `camel-itest` module. | 未調査 |  |
| C088 | 4.18.1→4.18.3 | パッチ | 高 | 破壊的変更(要確認) | camel-web3j | camel-web3j - potential breaking change | 原文全文を読み、該当するオプション/クラス/ヘッダー/URI を grep。使用していれば修正が必要 | camel-web3j, Web3jConstants, Web3jConstants.ID, ID, CamelWeb3jId, Web3jConstants.OPERATION, OPERATION, CamelWeb3jOperation, Web3jConstants.AT_BLOCK, AT_BLOCK, CamelWeb3jAtBlock, Web3jConstants.ADDRESS, ADDRESS, CamelWeb3jAddress | The Exchange header constants in `Web3jConstants` have been renamed to follow the Camel naming convention used across the rest of the component catalog. The Java field names are unchanged; only the header string values have changed: \| Constant \| Previous value \| New value \| `Web3jConstants.ID` \| `ID` \| `CamelWeb3jId` \| `Web3jConstants.OPERATION` \| `OPERATION` \| `CamelWeb3jOperation` \| `Web3jConstants.AT_BLOCK` \| `AT_BLOCK` \| `CamelWeb3jAtBlock` \| `Web3jConstants.ADDRESS` \| `ADDRESS` \| `CamelWeb3 | あり | The Exchange header constants in `Web3jConstants` have been renamed to follow the<br>Camel naming convention used across the rest of the component catalog. The Java<br>field names are unchanged; only the header string values have changed:<br><br>[options="header"]<br>\|===<br>\| Constant \| Previous value \| New value<br>\| `Web3jConstants.ID` \| `ID` \| `CamelWeb3jId`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.OPERATION` \| `OPERATION` \| `CamelWeb3jOperation`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.AT_BLOCK` \| `AT_BLOCK` \| `CamelWeb3jAtBlock`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.ADDRESS` \| `ADDRESS` \| `CamelWeb3jAddress`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.ADDRESSES` \| `ADDRESSES` \| `CamelWeb3jAddresses`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.FROM_ADDRESS` \| `FROM_ADDRESS` \| `CamelWeb3jFromAddress`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.TO_ADDRESS` \| `TO_ADDRESS` \| `CamelWeb3jToAddress`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.POSITION` \| `POSITION` \| `CamelWeb3jPosition`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.BLOCK_HASH` \| `BLOCK_HASH` \| `CamelWeb3jBlockHash`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.TRANSACTION_HASH` \| `TRANSACTION_HASH` \| `CamelWeb3jTransactionHash`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.SHA3_HASH_OF_DATA_TO_SIGN` \| `SHA3_HASH_OF_DATA_TO_SIGN` \| `CamelWeb3jSha3HashOfDataToSign`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.SIGNED_TRANSACTION_DATA` \| `SIGNED_TRANSACTION_DATA` \| `CamelWeb3jSignedTransactionData`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.FULL_TRANSACTION_OBJECTS` \| `FULL_TRANSACTION_OBJECTS` \| `CamelWeb3jFullTransactionObjects`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.INDEX` \| `INDEX` \| `CamelWeb3jIndex`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.SOURCE_CODE` \| `SOURCE_CODE` \| `CamelWeb3jSourceCode`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.FILTER_ID` \| `FILTER_ID` \| `CamelWeb3jFilterId`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.DATABASE_NAME` \| `DATABASE_NAME` \| `CamelWeb3jDatabaseName`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.KEY_NAME` \| `KEY_NAME` \| `CamelWeb3jKeyName`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.NONCE` \| `NONCE` \| `CamelWeb3jNonce`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.HEADER_POW_HASH` \| `HEADER_POW_HASH` \| `CamelWeb3jHeaderPowHash`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.MIX_DIGEST` \| `MIX_DIGEST` \| `CamelWeb3jMixDigest`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.CLIENT_ID` \| `CLIENT_ID` \| `CamelWeb3jClientId`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.GAS_PRICE` \| `GAS_PRICE` \| `CamelWeb3jGasPrice`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.GAS_LIMIT` \| `GAS_LIMIT` \| `CamelWeb3jGasLimit`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.VALUE` \| `VALUE` \| `CamelWeb3jValue`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.DATA` \| `DATA` \| `CamelWeb3jData`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.FROM_BLOCK` \| `FROM_BLOCK` \| `CamelWeb3jFromBlock`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.TO_BLOCK` \| `TO_BLOCK` \| `CamelWeb3jToBlock`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.TOPICS` \| `TOPICS` \| `CamelWeb3jTopics`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.PRIORITY` \| `PRIORITY` \| `CamelWeb3jPriority`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.TTL` \| `TTL` \| `CamelWeb3jTtl`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.PRIVATE_FOR` \| `PRIVATE_FOR` \| `CamelWeb3jPrivateFor`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.PRIVATE_FROM` \| `PRIVATE_FROM` \| `CamelWeb3jPrivateFrom`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.ERROR_CODE` \| `ERROR_CODE` \| `CamelWeb3jErrorCode`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.ERROR_DATA` \| `ERROR_DATA` \| `CamelWeb3jErrorData`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.ERROR_MESSAGE` \| `ERROR_MESSAGE` \| `CamelWeb3jErrorMessage`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.HEADER_STATUS` \| `status` \| `CamelWeb3jStatus`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.HEADER_OPERATION` \| `operation` \| `CamelWeb3jHeaderOperation`<br>\| `Web3jConstants.ETH_HASHRATE` \| `ETH_HASHRATE` \| `CamelWeb3jEthHashrate`<br>\|===<br><br>Routes that reference the constants symbolically (for example<br>`setHeader(Web3jConstants.FROM_ADDRESS, ...)`) continue to work without changes.<br>Routes that set the header by its literal string value (for example<br>`setHeader("FROM_ADDRESS", ...)`) must be updated to use the new value<br>(`setHeader("CamelWeb3jFromAddress", ...)`).<br><br>The `Web3jConstants.ETH_HASHRATE` constant is dual-purpose: it is both the<br>`CamelWeb3jOperation` value that dispatches the `ethHashrate` RPC and the header<br>name read by the `ETH_SUBMIT_HASHRATE` operation. Routes that referenced the<br>literal string `"ETH_HASHRATE"` (in either role) must be updated to<br>`"CamelWeb3jEthHashrate"`. Routes using the symbolic constant reference are<br>unaffected. The other producer-dispatch operation identifiers (`WEB3_CLIENT_VERSION`,<br>`ETH_GAS_PRICE`, `ETH_SEND_TRANSACTION`, ...) keep their previous string values<br>because they are operation enum values rather than Exchange header names.<br><br>As a consequence, the generated Endpoint DSL header accessors on<br>`Web3jHeaderNameBuilder` have been renamed accordingly:<br><br>* `iD()` -> `web3jId()`<br>* `atBlock()` -> `web3jAtBlock()`<br>* `aDDRESS()` -> `web3jAddress()`<br>* `aDDRESSES()` -> `web3jAddresses()`<br>* `fromAddress()` -> `web3jFromAddress()`<br>* `toAddress()` -> `web3jToAddress()`<br>* `pOSITION()` -> `web3jPosition()`<br>* `blockHash()` -> `web3jBlockHash()`<br>* `transactionHash()` -> `web3jTransactionHash()`<br>* `sha3HashOfDataToSign()` -> `web3jSha3HashOfDataToSign()`<br>* `signedTransactionData()` -> `web3jSignedTransactionData()`<br>* `fullTransactionObjects()` -> `web3jFullTransactionObjects()`<br>* `iNDEX()` -> `web3jIndex()`<br>* `sourceCode()` -> `web3jSourceCode()`<br>* `filterId()` -> `web3jFilterId()`<br>* `databaseName()` -> `web3jDatabaseName()`<br>* `keyName()` -> `web3jKeyName()`<br>* `nONCE()` -> `web3jNonce()`<br>* `headerPowHash()` -> `web3jHeaderPowHash()`<br>* `mixDigest()` -> `web3jMixDigest()`<br>* `clientId()` -> `web3jClientId()`<br>* `gasPrice()` -> `web3jGasPrice()`<br>* `gasLimit()` -> `web3jGasLimit()`<br>* `vALUE()` -> `web3jValue()`<br>* `dATA()` -> `web3jData()`<br>* `fromBlock()` -> `web3jFromBlock()`<br>* `toBlock()` -> `web3jToBlock()`<br>* `tOPICS()` -> `web3jTopics()`<br>* `pRIORITY()` -> `web3jPriority()`<br>* `tTL()` -> `web3jTtl()`<br>* `privateFor()` -> `web3jPrivateFor()`<br>* `privateFrom()` -> `web3jPrivateFrom()`<br>* `errorCode()` -> `web3jErrorCode()`<br>* `errorData()` -> `web3jErrorData()`<br>* `errorMessage()` -> `web3jErrorMessage()`<br>* `status()` -> `web3jStatus()`<br>* `operation()` -> `web3jHeaderOperation()`<br>* `ethHashrate()` -> `web3jEthHashrate()`<br><br>A new accessor `web3jOperation()` is also generated for `Web3jConstants.OPERATION`<br>(the producer dispatch header). This constant did not appear in the catalog<br>previously, so no DSL accessor renaming applies to it. | 未調査 |  |
| C089 | 4.18.1→4.18.3 | パッチ | 高 | 破壊的変更(要確認) | camel-milo | camel-milo - potential breaking change | 原文全文を読み、該当するオプション/クラス/ヘッダー/URI を grep。使用していれば修正が必要 | camel-milo, MiloConstants.HEADER_AWAIT, await, Camel, HeaderFilterStrategy, CamelMiloAwait, MiloConstants.HEADER_NODE_IDS, CamelMiloNodeIds, setHeader(MiloConstants.HEADER_AWAIT, ...), setHeader("await", ...), setHeader("CamelMiloAwait", ...), await(), MiloClientHeaderNameBuilder, miloAwait() | The `MiloConstants.HEADER_AWAIT` constant, which controls whether milo-client writes are awaited, used the header value `await` — outside the `Camel` namespace and therefore not filtered by the default `HeaderFilterStrategy`. It has been renamed to follow the Camel naming convention. The Java field name is unchanged; only the header string value has changed: \| Constant \| Previous value \| New value \| `MiloConstants.HEADER_AWAIT` \| `await` \| `CamelMiloAwait` `MiloConstants.HEADER_NODE_IDS` was alr | あり | The `MiloConstants.HEADER_AWAIT` constant, which controls whether milo-client<br>writes are awaited, used the header value `await` — outside the `Camel`<br>namespace and therefore not filtered by the default `HeaderFilterStrategy`. It<br>has been renamed to follow the Camel naming convention. The Java field name is<br>unchanged; only the header string value has changed:<br><br>[options="header"]<br>\|===<br>\| Constant \| Previous value \| New value<br>\| `MiloConstants.HEADER_AWAIT` \| `await` \| `CamelMiloAwait`<br>\|===<br><br>`MiloConstants.HEADER_NODE_IDS` was already `Camel`-prefixed<br>(`CamelMiloNodeIds`) and is unchanged.<br><br>Routes that reference the constant symbolically (for example<br>`setHeader(MiloConstants.HEADER_AWAIT, ...)`) continue to work without changes.<br>Routes that set the header by its literal string value (for example<br>`setHeader("await", ...)`) must be updated to use the new value<br>(`setHeader("CamelMiloAwait", ...)`).<br><br>As a consequence, the generated Endpoint DSL header accessor `await()` on<br>`MiloClientHeaderNameBuilder` has been renamed to `miloAwait()`. | 未調査 |  |
| C090 | 4.18.1→4.18.3 | パッチ | 高 | 破壊的変更(要確認) | camel-irc | camel-irc - potential breaking change | 原文全文を読み、該当するオプション/クラス/ヘッダー/URI を grep。使用していれば修正が必要 | camel-irc, IrcConstants, IrcConstants.IRC_MESSAGE_TYPE, irc.messageType, CamelIrcMessageType, IrcConstants.IRC_TARGET, irc.target, CamelIrcTarget, IrcConstants.IRC_SEND_TO, irc.sendTo, CamelIrcSendTo, IrcConstants.IRC_USER_KICKED, irc.user.kicked, CamelIrcUserKicked | The Exchange header constants in `IrcConstants` have been renamed to follow the Camel naming convention used across the rest of the component catalog. The Java field names are unchanged; only the header string values have changed: \| Constant \| Previous value \| New value \| `IrcConstants.IRC_MESSAGE_TYPE` \| `irc.messageType` \| `CamelIrcMessageType` \| `IrcConstants.IRC_TARGET` \| `irc.target` \| `CamelIrcTarget` \| `IrcConstants.IRC_SEND_TO` \| `irc.sendTo` \| `CamelIrcSendTo` \| `IrcConstants.IRC_USER_K | あり | The Exchange header constants in `IrcConstants` have been renamed to follow the<br>Camel naming convention used across the rest of the component catalog. The Java<br>field names are unchanged; only the header string values have changed:<br><br>[options="header"]<br>\|===<br>\| Constant \| Previous value \| New value<br>\| `IrcConstants.IRC_MESSAGE_TYPE`     \| `irc.messageType`     \| `CamelIrcMessageType`<br>\| `IrcConstants.IRC_TARGET`           \| `irc.target`          \| `CamelIrcTarget`<br>\| `IrcConstants.IRC_SEND_TO`          \| `irc.sendTo`          \| `CamelIrcSendTo`<br>\| `IrcConstants.IRC_USER_KICKED`      \| `irc.user.kicked`     \| `CamelIrcUserKicked`<br>\| `IrcConstants.IRC_USER_HOST`        \| `irc.user.host`       \| `CamelIrcUserHost`<br>\| `IrcConstants.IRC_USER_NICK`        \| `irc.user.nick`       \| `CamelIrcUserNick`<br>\| `IrcConstants.IRC_USER_SERVERNAME`  \| `irc.user.servername` \| `CamelIrcUserServername`<br>\| `IrcConstants.IRC_USER_USERNAME`    \| `irc.user.username`   \| `CamelIrcUserUsername`<br>\| `IrcConstants.IRC_NUM`              \| `irc.num`             \| `CamelIrcNum`<br>\| `IrcConstants.IRC_VALUE`            \| `irc.value`           \| `CamelIrcValue`<br>\|===<br><br>Routes that reference the constant symbolically (for example<br>`header(IrcConstants.IRC_SEND_TO)` or<br>`setHeader(IrcConstants.IRC_TARGET, ...)`) continue to work without changes.<br>Routes that set or read the header by its literal string value (for example<br>`setHeader("irc.sendTo", ...)`) must be updated to use the new value:<br><br>._Java-only: updating IRC header literal string values_<br>[source,java]<br>[code]<br>    // before<br>    template.sendBodyAndHeader("irc:bot@irc.server.org/#chan", "hello",<br>        "irc.sendTo", "#otherchan");<br>    <br>    // after<br>    template.sendBodyAndHeader("irc:bot@irc.server.org/#chan", "hello",<br>        "CamelIrcSendTo", "#otherchan"); | 未調査 |  |
| C091 | 4.18.1→4.18.3 | パッチ | 高 | ヘッダー名/フィルタ変更 | camel-mongodb-gridfs | camel-mongodb-gridfs | ヘッダー名の文字列リテラル、Constants 参照、headerFilterStrategy 設定を grep（新旧値は「Camelヘッダー値変更」シート）。外部システムとの連携ヘッダーにも注意 | camel-mongodb-gridfs, GridFsConstants, camel-mongodb-gridfs, camel-mongodb, MongoDbConstants.OPERATION_HEADER = "CamelMongoDbOperation", GridFsConstants.GRIDFS_OPERATION, GridFsConstants.GRIDFS_OBJECT_ID, gridfs.operation, CamelGridFsOperation, gridfs.metadata, CamelGridFsMetadata, gridfs.chunksize, CamelGridFsChunkSize, gridfs.objectid | The Exchange header values exposed by `GridFsConstants` have been renamed to follow the standard Camel naming convention, bringing `camel-mongodb-gridfs` in line with the parent `camel-mongodb` component (`MongoDbConstants.OPERATION_HEADER = "CamelMongoDbOperation"`). The Java field names are unchanged, so routes referencing the constants symbolically (e.g. `GridFsConstants.GRIDFS_OPERATION`, `GridFsConstants.GRIDFS_OBJECT_ID`) continue to work without modification. However, routes that set or r | あり | The Exchange header values exposed by `GridFsConstants` have been renamed to follow the standard<br>Camel naming convention, bringing `camel-mongodb-gridfs` in line with the parent `camel-mongodb`<br>component (`MongoDbConstants.OPERATION_HEADER = "CamelMongoDbOperation"`). The Java field names<br>are unchanged, so routes referencing the constants symbolically<br>(e.g. `GridFsConstants.GRIDFS_OPERATION`, `GridFsConstants.GRIDFS_OBJECT_ID`) continue to work<br>without modification. However, routes that set or read these headers using the raw string values<br>must be updated:<br><br>* `gridfs.operation` -> `CamelGridFsOperation`<br>* `gridfs.metadata` -> `CamelGridFsMetadata`<br>* `gridfs.chunksize` -> `CamelGridFsChunkSize`<br>* `gridfs.objectid` -> `CamelGridFsObjectId`<br>* `gridfs.fileid` -> `CamelGridFsFileId`<br><br>As a consequence, the generated Endpoint DSL header accessors on `GridFsHeaderNameBuilder`<br>have been renamed accordingly:<br><br>* `gridfsOperation()` -> `gridFsOperation()`<br>* `gridfsMetadata()` -> `gridFsMetadata()`<br>* `gridfsChunksize()` -> `gridFsChunkSize()`<br>* `gridfsObjectid()` -> `gridFsObjectId()`<br>* `gridfsFileid()` -> `gridFsFileId()` | 未調査 |  |
| C092 | 4.18.1→4.18.3 | パッチ | 高 | ヘッダー名/フィルタ変更 | camel-solr | camel-solr | ヘッダー名の文字列リテラル、Constants 参照、headerFilterStrategy 設定を grep（新旧値は「Camelヘッダー値変更」シート）。外部システムとの連携ヘッダーにも注意 | camel-solr, SolrConstants, SolrConstants.HEADER_FIELD_PREFIX, SolrField., CamelSolrField., SolrConstants.HEADER_PARAM_PREFIX, SolrParam., CamelSolrParam., setHeader("SolrField.id", ...), setHeader("SolrParam.commit", ...), CamelSolrField.id, CamelSolrParam.commit, Camel, HeaderFilterStrategy | The two Exchange header prefix constants in `SolrConstants` have been renamed to follow the Camel naming convention already used by the other constants in the same file (which were renamed in 4.10 under CAMEL-21697). The Java field names are unchanged; only the prefix string values have changed: \| Constant \| Previous value \| New value \| `SolrConstants.HEADER_FIELD_PREFIX` \| `SolrField.` \| `CamelSolrField.` \| `SolrConstants.HEADER_PARAM_PREFIX` \| `SolrParam.` \| `CamelSolrParam.` Routes that refer | あり | The two Exchange header prefix constants in `SolrConstants` have been renamed to<br>follow the Camel naming convention already used by the other constants in the<br>same file (which were renamed in 4.10 under CAMEL-21697). The Java field names<br>are unchanged; only the prefix string values have changed:<br><br>[options="header"]<br>\|===<br>\| Constant \| Previous value \| New value<br>\| `SolrConstants.HEADER_FIELD_PREFIX` \| `SolrField.` \| `CamelSolrField.`<br>\| `SolrConstants.HEADER_PARAM_PREFIX` \| `SolrParam.` \| `CamelSolrParam.`<br>\|===<br><br>Routes that reference the constants symbolically (for example<br>`setHeader(SolrConstants.HEADER_FIELD_PREFIX + "id", ...)`) continue to work<br>without changes. Routes that set the headers by their literal string value<br>(for example `setHeader("SolrField.id", ...)` or<br>`setHeader("SolrParam.commit", ...)`) must be updated to use the new prefix<br>(`CamelSolrField.id`, `CamelSolrParam.commit`).<br><br>Because the renamed prefixes now begin with `Camel`, they are stripped by the<br>standard transport `HeaderFilterStrategy` (`HttpHeaderFilterStrategy`, etc.)<br>when crossing a transport boundary, by design — `+Camel*+` headers are<br>framework-internal and are not propagated over the wire. Routes that bridge an<br>external transport (HTTP, JMS, ...) into a `solr:` producer and want to drive<br>Solr document fields or query parameters from a header supplied by the sender<br>must therefore carry the value in a non-`Camel`-prefixed application header and<br>map it to the appropriate `+CamelSolrField.*+` / `+CamelSolrParam.*+` header in the<br>route between the transport `from` and the `solr:` `to`. | 未調査 |  |
| C093 | 4.18.1→4.18.3 | パッチ | 高 | 破壊的変更(要確認) | camel-dapr | camel-dapr - potential breaking change | 原文全文を読み、該当するオプション/クラス/ヘッダー/URI を grep。使用していれば修正が必要 | camel-dapr, dapr, DaprHeaderFilterStrategy, DefaultHeaderFilterStrategy, headerFilterStrategy, camel-iggy, camel-kafka, camel-jms, Camel, camel, dapr-pubsub, pubsubName, topic, CamelDaprPubSubName | The `dapr` component now ships a default `DaprHeaderFilterStrategy` (extending `DefaultHeaderFilterStrategy`) and exposes it via the standard `headerFilterStrategy` endpoint/component option, aligning the component with the rest of the Camel component catalog (`camel-iggy`, `camel-kafka`, `camel-jms`, ...). The strategy filters headers starting with `Camel` / `camel` (case-insensitive) in both directions. In addition, the `dapr-pubsub` consumer no longer copies the inbound CloudEvent's `pubsubNa | あり | The `dapr` component now ships a default `DaprHeaderFilterStrategy` (extending<br>`DefaultHeaderFilterStrategy`) and exposes it via the standard `headerFilterStrategy`<br>endpoint/component option, aligning the component with the rest of the Camel component<br>catalog (`camel-iggy`, `camel-kafka`, `camel-jms`, ...). The strategy filters headers<br>starting with `Camel` / `camel` (case-insensitive) in both directions.<br><br>In addition, the `dapr-pubsub` consumer no longer copies the inbound CloudEvent's<br>`pubsubName` and `topic` into the `CamelDaprPubSubName` (`DaprConstants.PUBSUB_NAME`)<br>and `CamelDaprTopic` (`DaprConstants.TOPIC`) message headers. These two constants are<br>producer-direction routing headers: they are read back on the producer side by<br>`DaprConfigurationOptionsProxy` and take precedence over the endpoint-configured<br>`pubSubName` / `topic`. Setting them on a consumed exchange caused a route such as<br><br>._Java-only: Dapr pubsub route affected by header change_<br>[source,java]<br>[code]<br>    from("dapr-pubsub:configured-pubsub:configured-topic")<br>        .to("dapr-pubsub:configured-pubsub:another-topic");<br><br>to carry the inbound pubsubName / topic into the producer hop instead of using the<br>configured destination. The remaining CloudEvent metadata headers (`CamelDaprID`,<br>`CamelDaprSource`, `CamelDaprType`, `CamelDaprSpecificVersion`,<br>`CamelDaprDataContentType`, `CamelDaprBinaryData`, `CamelDaprTime`,<br>`CamelDaprTraceParent`, `CamelDaprTraceState`) are unchanged and are still set on the<br>inbound exchange.<br><br>Because a `dapr-pubsub` consumer subscribes to a single, fixed `pubSubName` / `topic`,<br>the removed headers were redundant with the endpoint configuration. Routes that relied<br>on reading `CamelDaprPubSubName` / `CamelDaprTopic` from a consumed exchange should read<br>the configured destination from the endpoint URI instead. | 未調査 |  |
| C094 | 4.18.1→4.18.3 | パッチ | 高 | 破壊的変更(要確認) | camel-schematron | camel-schematron - potential breaking change | 原文全文を読み、該当するオプション/クラス/ヘッダー/URI を grep。使用していれば修正が必要 | camel-schematron, TransformerFactory, FEATURE_SECURE_PROCESSING, accessExternalDTD, accessExternalStylesheet, SAXParserFactory, URIResolver | The Schematron rules-compilation `TransformerFactory` now runs with secure processing enabled (`FEATURE_SECURE_PROCESSING`) and with external DTD and external stylesheet access disabled (`accessExternalDTD` and `accessExternalStylesheet` set to empty), as defense-in-depth against XXE and external-resource resolution while compiling Schematron rules. This matches the hardening already applied to the component's `SAXParserFactory`. The bundled ISO Schematron skeleton stylesheets continue to be res | あり | The Schematron rules-compilation `TransformerFactory` now runs with secure processing enabled<br>(`FEATURE_SECURE_PROCESSING`) and with external DTD and external stylesheet access disabled<br>(`accessExternalDTD` and `accessExternalStylesheet` set to empty), as defense-in-depth against XXE and<br>external-resource resolution while compiling Schematron rules. This matches the hardening already applied<br>to the component's `SAXParserFactory`. The bundled ISO Schematron skeleton stylesheets continue to be<br>resolved from the classpath via the component's `URIResolver` and are therefore unaffected.<br><br>If your Schematron rules legitimately reference an external DTD, external entity, or external stylesheet,<br>those references will no longer be resolved and rule compilation will fail; inline the referenced content<br>instead. | 未調査 |  |
| C095 | 4.18.3→4.18.4 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-core | camel-core - Multicast EIP honors UseOriginalAggregationStrategy | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-core, UseOriginalAggregationStrategy, Splitter, RecipientListProcessor, MulticastProcessor, newInstance(exchange) | The `UseOriginalAggregationStrategy` binding has been moved from `Splitter` and `RecipientListProcessor` into `MulticastProcessor` (their shared base class), so all three EIPs (Multicast, Splitter, RecipientList) now consistently bind the original exchange on the strategy. Previously, Multicast never called `newInstance(exchange)`, which made the strategy silently ineffective — especially in error scenarios where the aggregated result could overwrite the original exchange body instead of preserv |  | The `UseOriginalAggregationStrategy` binding has been moved from `Splitter` and `RecipientListProcessor`<br>into `MulticastProcessor` (their shared base class), so all three EIPs (Multicast, Splitter,<br>RecipientList) now consistently bind the original exchange on the strategy. Previously, Multicast<br>never called `newInstance(exchange)`, which made the strategy silently ineffective — especially in<br>error scenarios where the aggregated result could overwrite the original exchange body instead of<br>preserving it. | 未調査 |  |
| C096 | 4.18.3→4.18.4 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-azure-servicebus | camel-azure-servicebus - Camel-managed message lock renewal | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-azure-servicebus, PEEK_LOCK, maxAutoLockRenewDuration > 0, ServiceBusReceiverAsyncClient, PeriodTaskScheduler, processMessage, processorClient, maxAutoLockRenewDuration | When consuming from Azure Service Bus in `PEEK_LOCK` mode with `maxAutoLockRenewDuration > 0`, Camel now actively renews message locks using a dedicated `ServiceBusReceiverAsyncClient` and Camel's internal `PeriodTaskScheduler`. This addresses a limitation where the Azure SDK's built-in lock renewal is tied to the `processMessage` callback duration, which returns immediately for asynchronous Camel routes — causing long-running exchanges to silently lose their message locks. The new behavior acti | あり | When consuming from Azure Service Bus in `PEEK_LOCK` mode with `maxAutoLockRenewDuration > 0`, Camel now<br>actively renews message locks using a dedicated `ServiceBusReceiverAsyncClient` and Camel's internal<br>`PeriodTaskScheduler`. This addresses a limitation where the Azure SDK's built-in lock renewal is tied<br>to the `processMessage` callback duration, which returns immediately for asynchronous Camel routes —<br>causing long-running exchanges to silently lose their message locks.<br><br>The new behavior activates automatically when all of the following are true:<br><br>* No custom `processorClient` is provided<br>* Receive mode is `PEEK_LOCK`<br>* `maxAutoLockRenewDuration > 0`<br>* Session mode is disabled<br><br>No configuration changes are required. The existing `maxAutoLockRenewDuration` option controls how long<br>Camel will continue renewing a message's lock. | 未調査 |  |
| C097 | 4.18.3→4.18.4 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-http | camel-http - gzip double decompression with HttpClient 5.6+ | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-http, Content-Encoding, Content-Length, Content-MD5, entity.getContentEncoding(), ZipException: Not in GZIP format | When using HttpClient 5.6+ (including transitively via a Spring Boot BOM on Camel 4.18.x), HttpClient auto-decompresses gzip response bodies but may leave stale `Content-Encoding`, `Content-Length`, and `Content-MD5` headers. Camel now reads content encoding from `entity.getContentEncoding()` instead of the response header and strips stale compression headers after the HTTP call. This prevents a second decompression attempt that could fail with `ZipException: Not in GZIP format` against gzip-com |  | When using HttpClient 5.6+ (including transitively via a Spring Boot BOM on Camel 4.18.x), HttpClient<br>auto-decompresses gzip response bodies but may leave stale `Content-Encoding`, `Content-Length`, and<br>`Content-MD5` headers. Camel now reads content encoding from `entity.getContentEncoding()` instead of<br>the response header and strips stale compression headers after the HTTP call. This prevents a second<br>decompression attempt that could fail with `ZipException: Not in GZIP format` against gzip-compressing<br>servers with default settings. | 未調査 |  |
| C098 | 4.18.3→4.18.4 | パッチ | 高 | ヘッダー名/フィルタ変更 | camel-mail | camel-mail - MimeMultipartDataFormat inbound header filtering | ヘッダー名の文字列リテラル、Constants 参照、headerFilterStrategy 設定を grep（新旧値は「Camelヘッダー値変更」シート）。外部システムとの連携ヘッダーにも注意 | camel-mail, headersInline=true, mime-multipart, HeaderFilterStrategy, Camel* | When unmarshalling a MIME message with `headersInline=true`, the `mime-multipart` data format now applies a `HeaderFilterStrategy` to the headers copied from the MIME content onto the Camel message. Camel-internal headers (the `Camel*` namespace, matched case-insensitively) present in the external MIME headers are no longer copied onto the message, consistent with the inbound header filtering already performed by the camel-mail consumer. Ordinary application headers are unaffected. If a route re | あり | When unmarshalling a MIME message with `headersInline=true`, the `mime-multipart` data format now applies a<br>`HeaderFilterStrategy` to the headers copied from the MIME content onto the Camel message. Camel-internal headers<br>(the `Camel*` namespace, matched case-insensitively) present in the external MIME headers are no longer copied onto<br>the message, consistent with the inbound header filtering already performed by the camel-mail consumer.<br><br>Ordinary application headers are unaffected. If a route relied on `Camel*` headers being propagated from the MIME<br>content, set them explicitly after unmarshalling. | 未調査 |  |
| C099 | 4.18.3→4.18.4 | パッチ | 高 | ヘッダー名/フィルタ変更 | camel-knative | camel-knative - structured-mode CloudEvent header filtering | ヘッダー名の文字列リテラル、Constants 参照、headerFilterStrategy 設定を grep（新旧値は「Camelヘッダー値変更」シート）。外部システムとの連携ヘッダーにも注意 | camel-knative, HeaderFilterStrategy, Camel* | When consuming a CloudEvent in structured content mode (`application/cloudevents+json`), the Knative component now applies a `HeaderFilterStrategy` to the event fields (extensions) mapped from the payload onto the Camel message. Camel-internal headers (the `Camel*` namespace, matched case-insensitively) present as structured-event fields are no longer mapped onto the message, consistent with the inbound header filtering already performed on the binary content-mode / HTTP header path. Ordinary Cl | あり | When consuming a CloudEvent in structured content mode (`application/cloudevents+json`), the Knative component now<br>applies a `HeaderFilterStrategy` to the event fields (extensions) mapped from the payload onto the Camel message.<br>Camel-internal headers (the `Camel*` namespace, matched case-insensitively) present as structured-event fields are<br>no longer mapped onto the message, consistent with the inbound header filtering already performed on the binary<br>content-mode / HTTP header path.<br><br>Ordinary CloudEvent extension attributes are unaffected. If a route relied on `Camel*`-named fields being<br>propagated from the structured payload, set them explicitly after consuming the event. | 未調査 |  |
| C100 | 4.18.3→4.18.4 | パッチ | 高 | ヘッダー名/フィルタ変更 | camel-ironmq | camel-ironmq - message envelope header filtering | ヘッダー名の文字列リテラル、Constants 参照、headerFilterStrategy 設定を grep（新旧値は「Camelヘッダー値変更」シート）。外部システムとの連携ヘッダーにも注意 | camel-ironmq, preserveHeaders=true, HeaderFilterStrategy, Camel* | When consuming a message with `preserveHeaders=true`, the IronMQ consumer now applies a `HeaderFilterStrategy` to the header entries embedded in the JSON message envelope before mapping them onto the Camel message. Camel-internal headers (the `Camel*` namespace, matched case-insensitively) present in the envelope are no longer mapped onto the message, consistent with the inbound header filtering performed by other consumers. Ordinary application headers are unaffected. If a route relied on `Came | あり | When consuming a message with `preserveHeaders=true`, the IronMQ consumer now applies a `HeaderFilterStrategy` to<br>the header entries embedded in the JSON message envelope before mapping them onto the Camel message. Camel-internal<br>headers (the `Camel*` namespace, matched case-insensitively) present in the envelope are no longer mapped onto the<br>message, consistent with the inbound header filtering performed by other consumers.<br><br>Ordinary application headers are unaffected. If a route relied on `Camel*` headers being propagated from the<br>message envelope, set them explicitly after consuming the message. | 未調査 |  |
| C101 | 4.18.3→4.18.4 | パッチ | 高 | ヘッダー名/フィルタ変更 | camel-azure-eventhubs | camel-azure-eventhubs - producer now filters Camel-internal headers | ヘッダー名の文字列リテラル、Constants 参照、headerFilterStrategy 設定を grep（新旧値は「Camelヘッダー値変更」シート）。外部システムとの連携ヘッダーにも注意 | camel-azure-eventhubs, azure-eventhubs, DefaultHeaderFilterStrategy, EventData, Camel*, camel-azure-eventhubs | The `azure-eventhubs` producer now applies a `DefaultHeaderFilterStrategy` to the headers copied onto `EventData` application properties. Camel-internal headers (the `Camel*` namespace, matched case-insensitively) are no longer forwarded to Azure Event Hubs, consistent with the inbound header filtering performed by other components. Ordinary application headers are unaffected. NOTE: The filter was not installed in 4.18.4, so the producer still copied every header. It is applied from 4.18.5, see | あり | The `azure-eventhubs` producer now applies a `DefaultHeaderFilterStrategy` to the headers copied onto<br>`EventData` application properties. Camel-internal headers (the `Camel*` namespace, matched<br>case-insensitively) are no longer forwarded to Azure Event Hubs, consistent with the inbound header<br>filtering performed by other components.<br><br>Ordinary application headers are unaffected.<br><br>NOTE: The filter was not installed in 4.18.4, so the producer still copied every header. It is applied from<br>4.18.5, see the 4.18.5 entry for `camel-azure-eventhubs` (CAMEL-25227). | 未調査 |  |
| C102 | 4.18.3→4.18.4 | パッチ | 高 | 破壊的変更(要確認) | camel-atmosphere-websocket | camel-atmosphere-websocket - potential breaking change | 原文全文を読み、該当するオプション/クラス/ヘッダー/URI を grep。使用していれば修正が必要 | camel-atmosphere-websocket, WebsocketConstants, WebsocketConstants.CONNECTION_KEY, websocket.connectionKey, CamelAtmosphereWebsocketConnectionKey, WebsocketConstants.CONNECTION_KEY_LIST, websocket.connectionKey.list, CamelAtmosphereWebsocketConnectionKeyList, WebsocketConstants.SEND_TO_ALL, websocket.sendToAll, CamelAtmosphereWebsocketSendToAll, WebsocketConstants.EVENT_TYPE, websocket.eventType, CamelAtmosphereWebsocketEventType | The Exchange header constants in `WebsocketConstants` have been renamed to follow the Camel naming convention used across the rest of the component catalog. The Java field names are unchanged; only the header string values have changed: \| Constant \| Previous value \| New value \| `WebsocketConstants.CONNECTION_KEY` \| `websocket.connectionKey` \| `CamelAtmosphereWebsocketConnectionKey` \| `WebsocketConstants.CONNECTION_KEY_LIST` \| `websocket.connectionKey.list` \| `CamelAtmosphereWebsocketConnectionKe | あり | The Exchange header constants in `WebsocketConstants` have been renamed to follow the<br>Camel naming convention used across the rest of the component catalog. The Java field<br>names are unchanged; only the header string values have changed:<br><br>[options="header"]<br>\|===<br>\| Constant \| Previous value \| New value<br>\| `WebsocketConstants.CONNECTION_KEY`      \| `websocket.connectionKey`      \| `CamelAtmosphereWebsocketConnectionKey`<br>\| `WebsocketConstants.CONNECTION_KEY_LIST` \| `websocket.connectionKey.list` \| `CamelAtmosphereWebsocketConnectionKeyList`<br>\| `WebsocketConstants.SEND_TO_ALL`         \| `websocket.sendToAll`          \| `CamelAtmosphereWebsocketSendToAll`<br>\| `WebsocketConstants.EVENT_TYPE`          \| `websocket.eventType`          \| `CamelAtmosphereWebsocketEventType`<br>\| `WebsocketConstants.ERROR_TYPE`          \| `websocket.errorType`          \| `CamelAtmosphereWebsocketErrorType`<br>\|===<br><br>Routes that reference the constant symbolically (for example<br>`setHeader(WebsocketConstants.CONNECTION_KEY, ...)`) continue to work without changes.<br>Routes that set the header by its literal string value must be updated to use the new<br>value. | 未調査 |  |
| C103 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 高 | ヘッダー名/フィルタ変更 | camel-azure-eventhubs | camel-azure-eventhubs | ヘッダー名の文字列リテラル、Constants 参照、headerFilterStrategy 設定を grep（新旧値は「Camelヘッダー値変更」シート）。外部システムとの連携ヘッダーにも注意 | camel-azure-eventhubs, Camel*, EventData, headerFilterStrategy, CamelAzureEventHubsPartitionKey, CamelAzureEventHubsPartitionId, Both partitionKey and partitionId are set, partitionKey, partitionId, CamelAzureEventHubsReceivedPartitionId | The producer now leaves the Camel-internal headers (`Camel*`) out of the `EventData` application properties by default. The 4.18.4 upgrade guide already announced this, but the filter was not installed, so 4.18.4 and earlier 4.18 releases still copied every header. Configure a custom `headerFilterStrategy` on the component to change which headers are sent. A route that consumes from one Event Hub and produces to another no longer fails, or ignores the producer configuration, because of the `Came | あり | The producer now leaves the Camel-internal headers (`Camel*`) out of the `EventData` application properties by<br>default. The 4.18.4 upgrade guide already announced this, but the filter was not installed, so 4.18.4 and earlier 4.18<br>releases still copied every header. Configure a custom `headerFilterStrategy` on the component<br>to change which headers are sent.<br><br>A route that consumes from one Event Hub and produces to another no longer fails, or ignores the producer<br>configuration, because of the `CamelAzureEventHubsPartitionKey` and `CamelAzureEventHubsPartitionId` headers that the<br>consumer sets. Such a route used to fail with `Both partitionKey and partitionId are set` for every event with a<br>partition key, and for every event when `partitionKey` was configured, and a configured `partitionId` was replaced by<br>the partition the event was received from. The producer now picks the partition from, in this order: a partition<br>header set by the route, the `partitionKey` or `partitionId` endpoint option, and the partition key of the received<br>event. It no longer reuses the partition id of the received event, so an event without a partition key is no longer<br>sent to the partition with the same id in the target Event Hub. To keep doing so, for example when mirroring between<br>Event Hubs that have the same partitions, remove the `CamelAzureEventHubsReceivedPartitionId` exchange property before<br>the producer. | 未調査 |  |
| C104 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-zipfile, camel-tarfile | camel-zipfile, camel-tarfile - a maxDecompressedSize of 2 GiB or more is now enforced | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-zipfile, camel-tarfile, maxDecompressedSize, ZipSplitter, Integer.MAX_VALUE, IOException, -1, IOHelper.copy(InputStream, OutputStream, int, boolean, long) | The `maxDecompressedSize` option of the Zip File and Tar File data formats (and of `ZipSplitter`) was not enforced when set to 2 GiB (`Integer.MAX_VALUE` bytes) or more, because the byte count behind the check wrapped around. The limit is now enforced for any value: unmarshalling an entry that decompresses to more than the configured `maxDecompressedSize` fails with an `IOException`, as documented. The default limit (1 GiB) is unchanged, and `-1` still disables the limit. `IOHelper.copy(InputStr | あり | The `maxDecompressedSize` option of the Zip File and Tar File data formats (and of `ZipSplitter`) was not<br>enforced when set to 2 GiB (`Integer.MAX_VALUE` bytes) or more, because the byte count behind the check<br>wrapped around. The limit is now enforced for any value: unmarshalling an entry that decompresses to more<br>than the configured `maxDecompressedSize` fails with an `IOException`, as documented. The default limit<br>(1 GiB) is unchanged, and `-1` still disables the limit.<br><br>`IOHelper.copy(InputStream, OutputStream, int, boolean, long)` now returns `Integer.MAX_VALUE`, instead of a<br>negative number, when it copies more than `Integer.MAX_VALUE` bytes. | 未調査 |  |
| C105 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-oauth | camel-oauth - the post login url is confined to the configured redirect uri origin | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-oauth, scheme://host[:port], camel.oauth.redirect-uri, OAuthCodeFlowProcessor, X-Forwarded-Proto, X-Forwarded-Host, X-Forwarded-Port, Host, CamelHttpUrl | The post login url of the authorization code flow is now always built from the origin (`scheme://host[:port]`) of the configured `camel.oauth.redirect-uri`, plus the requested path. `OAuthCodeFlowProcessor` previously rebuilt that url from the `X-Forwarded-Proto` / `X-Forwarded-Host` / `X-Forwarded-Port` request headers, or from the `Host` header behind `CamelHttpUrl` when those were absent. All of those are set by the caller, so a request could point the post login redirect at any origin. A dep | あり | The post login url of the authorization code flow is now always built from the origin (`scheme://host[:port]`)<br>of the configured `camel.oauth.redirect-uri`, plus the requested path. `OAuthCodeFlowProcessor` previously<br>rebuilt that url from the `X-Forwarded-Proto` / `X-Forwarded-Host` / `X-Forwarded-Port` request headers, or<br>from the `Host` header behind `CamelHttpUrl` when those were absent. All of those are set by the caller, so<br>a request could point the post login redirect at any origin. A deployment behind an ingress or an OpenShift<br>Route still redirects to its externally reachable address, because `camel.oauth.redirect-uri` is the address<br>the identity provider sends the browser back to. Deployments whose external address differs from that<br>property must set it to the address the browser actually reaches; a request announcing another origin is now<br>redirected to the configured one and a warning is logged. | 未調査 |  |
| C106 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 高 | ヘッダー名/フィルタ変更 | camel-spring-ws | camel-spring-ws - internal Camel headers are now filtered when mapping SOAP response headers | ヘッダー名の文字列リテラル、Constants 参照、headerFilterStrategy 設定を grep（新旧値は「Camelヘッダー値変更」シート）。外部システムとの連携ヘッダーにも注意 | camel-spring-ws, allowResponseHeaderOverride, spring-ws, headerFilterStrategy, SpringWebserviceHeaderFilterStrategy, Camel, camel, CamelSpringWebserviceSoapHeader | When `allowResponseHeaderOverride` is enabled, the `spring-ws` producer now applies the endpoint's `headerFilterStrategy` to the SOAP response header attributes and elements it maps onto the message, as the `spring-ws` consumer already does for inbound SOAP headers. With the default `SpringWebserviceHeaderFilterStrategy`, attribute and element names starting with `Camel` or `camel` (case-insensitively) are no longer copied onto the message. Other SOAP response header attributes and elements are | あり | When `allowResponseHeaderOverride` is enabled, the `spring-ws` producer now applies the endpoint's<br>`headerFilterStrategy` to the SOAP response header attributes and elements it maps onto the message, as the<br>`spring-ws` consumer already does for inbound SOAP headers. With the default `SpringWebserviceHeaderFilterStrategy`,<br>attribute and element names starting with `Camel` or `camel` (case-insensitively) are no longer copied onto the<br>message.<br><br>Other SOAP response header attributes and elements are mapped as before, and the raw SOAP header is still<br>available in the `CamelSpringWebserviceSoapHeader` header. A route that relies on the previous behaviour can supply a<br>custom `headerFilterStrategy` on the `spring-ws` endpoint. | 未調査 |  |
| C107 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-http-common, camel-platform-http-vertx | camel-http-common, camel-platform-http-vertx - fileNameExtWhitelist entries are matched exactly | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-http-common, camel-platform-http-vertx, fileNameExtWhitelist, camel-http-base, txt, evil.x, camel-platform-http-vertx, DefaultHttpBinding, camel-http-common | `fileNameExtWhitelist` is now checked the same way everywhere, by one shared check in `camel-http-base`. Two places still matched each extension as a substring of the whitelist, so for example a whitelist of `txt` accepted an upload named `evil.x`: the `camel-platform-http-vertx` consumer, and `DefaultHttpBinding` in `camel-http-common` for uploads that arrive as request attributes, which also replaced the configured `fileNameExtWhitelist` with its lower-cased value. Both now compare each comma- | あり | `fileNameExtWhitelist` is now checked the same way everywhere, by one shared check in `camel-http-base`. Two<br>places still matched each extension as a substring of the whitelist, so for example a whitelist of `txt`<br>accepted an upload named `evil.x`: the `camel-platform-http-vertx` consumer, and `DefaultHttpBinding` in<br>`camel-http-common` for uploads that arrive as request attributes, which also replaced the configured<br>`fileNameExtWhitelist` with its lower-cased value. Both now compare each comma-separated entry exactly and<br>case-insensitively. `*` still accepts every file, a file name without an extension is still accepted, and the<br>configured value is left unchanged. An upload whose extension only matched as part of a longer entry is now<br>rejected. | 未調査 |  |
| C108 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-core | camel-core - masking of sensitive values in endpoint URIs | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-core, URISupport.sanitizeUri(), access-key, accessKey, RAW(...), RAW{...}, Unknown parameters=[...], camel.main.additionalSensitiveKeywords, URISupport.addSanitizeKeywords() | `URISupport.sanitizeUri()`, which masks secrets in endpoint URIs shown in logs, events, JMX names and error messages, now masks some values it used to miss, so the masked form of a URI can differ from earlier releases: * option names are matched ignoring dashes, so `access-key` is masked like `accessKey` * a `RAW(...)` or `RAW{...}` value is masked up to the bracket that ends it, also when the value contains that bracket * the userinfo password of every URI in a text is masked, not only the last | あり | `URISupport.sanitizeUri()`, which masks secrets in endpoint URIs shown in logs, events, JMX names and error<br>messages, now masks some values it used to miss, so the masked form of a URI can differ from earlier releases:<br><br>* option names are matched ignoring dashes, so `access-key` is masked like `accessKey`<br>* a `RAW(...)` or `RAW{...}` value is masked up to the bracket that ends it, also when the value contains that bracket<br>* the userinfo password of every URI in a text is masked, not only the last one<br>* the `Unknown parameters=[...]` message of an endpoint that cannot be created masks the values of sensitive parameters<br><br>The keywords added with `camel.main.additionalSensitiveKeywords` (or `URISupport.addSanitizeKeywords()`) now apply to<br>every matching parameter, not only the first one, and they are added to the keywords configured earlier instead of<br>replacing them. The keywords apply to the whole JVM and cannot be removed. | 未調査 |  |
| C109 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-core | camel-core - Rest DSL response Content-Type | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-core, produces, application/json, application/xml, text/plain, */* | Under json or xml binding, a response without a Content-Type header again takes it from the `produces` of the rest verb, as before Camel 4.18.4 and 4.22.0. Those releases used `application/json` (or `application/xml`) whenever the binding mode allowed it and marshalled the body, so a verb producing `text/plain` answered with a json-quoted body and a binary body failed. When `produces` lists several media types, the first json (or xml) type is used, otherwise the first one. Wildcards such as `*/* | あり | Under json or xml binding, a response without a Content-Type header again takes it from the `produces` of the rest<br>verb, as before Camel 4.18.4 and 4.22.0. Those releases used `application/json` (or `application/xml`) whenever the<br>binding mode allowed it and marshalled the body, so a verb producing `text/plain` answered with a json-quoted body and<br>a binary body failed. When `produces` lists several media types, the first json (or xml) type is used, otherwise the<br>first one. Wildcards such as `*/*` are skipped, falling back to `application/json` or `application/xml`. | 未調査 |  |
| C110 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-docling | camel-docling | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-docling, String,  or contained , allowUrlSource, false, allowFilePathSource, , or containing , IllegalArgumentException, CamelDoclingInputFilePath, File, byte[], InputStream, List<String>, String[] | A `String` message body is no longer interpreted as a location by default. Previously the producer inspected the body and, when it started with `http://` or `https://`, handed it to Docling as a remote URL to fetch; when it started with `/` or contained `\`, it read it from the local filesystem; otherwise it converted it as document content. The two location readings must now be enabled explicitly: * `allowUrlSource` (default `false`) - interpret a body starting with `http://` or `https://` as a | あり | A `String` message body is no longer interpreted as a location by default. Previously the producer<br>inspected the body and, when it started with `http://` or `https://`, handed it to Docling as a remote<br>URL to fetch; when it started with `/` or contained `\`, it read it from the local filesystem; otherwise<br>it converted it as document content.<br><br>The two location readings must now be enabled explicitly:<br><br>* `allowUrlSource` (default `false`) - interpret a body starting with `http://` or `https://` as a URL.<br>* `allowFilePathSource` (default `false`) - interpret a body starting with `/`, or containing `\`, as a<br>  local file path. This also covers the single directory-or-file `String` body accepted by the batch<br>  operations.<br><br>A route that passes the document itself in the body is unaffected. A route that passes a URL or a path in<br>the body must set the matching option, otherwise the exchange fails with an `IllegalArgumentException`<br>naming the option to enable.<br><br>The `CamelDoclingInputFilePath` header is unchanged and still accepts a path without any opt-in, as are<br>`File`, `byte[]` and `InputStream` bodies and the explicit path collections (`List<String>`, `String[]`,<br>`List<File>`, `File[]`) used by the batch operations.<br><br>A new `inputBaseDirectory` option is also available. When set, every local input path - from the header,<br>from a file path body, and from the batch operations - must resolve inside that directory once normalized.<br>It is unset by default, which keeps the previous behaviour of accepting any path.<br><br>Additionally, a local input path that does not exist is now reported as a `File not found` `IOException`<br>before Docling is invoked. Previously the size check silently skipped a path that resolved to nothing and<br>the failure surfaced later, from the Docling process or API call.<br><br>The `CamelDoclingOutputFilePath` header, which selects the CLI output directory, is now normalized lexically<br>(redundant separators and `.`/`..` segments are resolved) before it is passed to Docling; a header value<br>without such segments is still used as given. A new `outputBaseDirectory` option additionally confines the<br>header the same way `inputBaseDirectory` confines input paths - when set, an output path that resolves outside<br>it, including an absolute path, is rejected with an `IOException`. `outputBaseDirectory` is unset by default. | 未調査 |  |
| C111 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-dynamic-router | camel-dynamic-router | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-dynamic-router, dynamic-router-control, predicate, expressionLanguage, IllegalArgumentException, predicateBean, Predicate, allowedSchemes, dynamic-router, security = "insecure:dev", allowPredicateFromMessage | The `dynamic-router-control` endpoint no longer takes the subscription `predicate`, or the `expressionLanguage` used to compile it, from the incoming control message. A control message that supplies either is now rejected with an `IllegalArgumentException`. The predicate is compiled and then evaluated against every exchange on the channel, so letting the control message choose both the language and the expression means the sender of that message decides what runs inside the Camel process. Only e | あり | The `dynamic-router-control` endpoint no longer takes the subscription `predicate`, or the<br>`expressionLanguage` used to compile it, from the incoming control message. A control message that<br>supplies either is now rejected with an `IllegalArgumentException`.<br><br>The predicate is compiled and then evaluated against every exchange on the channel, so letting the<br>control message choose both the language and the expression means the sender of that message decides<br>what runs inside the Camel process. Only enable this when control messages can only come from a<br>trusted source:<br><br>[source,java]<br>[code]<br>    from("kafka:subscriptions")<br>        .unmarshal().json(DynamicRouterControlMessage.class)<br>        .to("dynamic-router-control:subscribe?allowPredicateFromMessage=true");<br><br>Two alternatives avoid the flag entirely. A control message may still name a `predicateBean`, which<br>selects a `Predicate` that the route author bound in the registry; that path is unchanged. The<br>control endpoint may also carry `predicate` and `expressionLanguage` as URI parameters, in which case<br>every subscription made through that endpoint uses the route author's expression. Subscription<br>parameters that the control message does not carry now fall back to the values configured on the<br>endpoint.<br><br>Note that this release line does not carry the `allowedSchemes` option added to the `dynamic-router`<br>endpoint on newer lines, nor the `security = "insecure:dev"` marker on `allowPredicateFromMessage`,<br>because neither the option allow-list nor the security policy framework exists in 4.18. | 未調査 |  |
| C112 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-ftp, camel-sftp, camel-ftps, camel-mina-sftp, camel-azure-files, camel-smb | camel-ftp, camel-sftp, camel-ftps, camel-mina-sftp, camel-azure-files, camel-smb | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-ftp, camel-sftp, camel-ftps, camel-mina-sftp, camel-azure-files, camel-smb, ../, jailStartingDirectory, true, localWorkDirectory, jailStartingDirectory=false, fileName, useList=false | The remote-file consumers now ensure the path resolved for a polled file stays within the directory being polled. The file name that path is built from is reported by the remote server in its directory listing and is not guaranteed to be a single path segment, so a listing entry containing `../` sequences could previously resolve to a path outside the configured directory and be used as the operand for retrieving, deleting or renaming a file. The containment check honours the existing `jailStart | あり | The remote-file consumers now ensure the path resolved for a polled file stays within the directory being<br>polled. The file name that path is built from is reported by the remote server in its directory listing and is<br>not guaranteed to be a single path segment, so a listing entry containing `../` sequences could previously<br>resolve to a path outside the configured directory and be used as the operand for retrieving, deleting or<br>renaming a file.<br><br>The containment check honours the existing `jailStartingDirectory` option (default `true`), consistent with the<br>file producer and with the `localWorkDirectory` download path; set `jailStartingDirectory=false` to disable it.<br>A file that resolves outside the configured directory is now skipped, and a warning is logged.<br><br>Ordinary listings are unaffected, as a listed name is normally a single path segment, and a `../` that still<br>resolves back inside the polled directory remains accepted. Two configurations can newly see files skipped: a<br>server that reports names navigating above the polled directory, and a `fileName` expression (used when<br>`useList=false`) that navigates above it. Set `jailStartingDirectory=false` if such a path is intended. | 未調査 |  |
| C113 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-azure-storage-blob, camel-azure-storage-datalake | camel-azure-storage-blob and camel-azure-storage-datalake | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-azure-storage-blob, camel-azure-storage-datalake, fileDir | Local downloads configured with `fileDir` now resolve existing filesystem path segments before checking that the destination remains inside the configured directory. Downloads through a symbolic link that resolves outside `fileDir` are rejected. Valid nested download paths continue to work. | あり | Local downloads configured with `fileDir` now resolve existing filesystem path segments before checking<br>that the destination remains inside the configured directory. Downloads through a symbolic link that<br>resolves outside `fileDir` are rejected. Valid nested download paths continue to work. | 未調査 |  |
| C114 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-google-storage | camel-google-storage | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-google-storage, downloadFileName | Local downloads configured with a plain `downloadFileName` directory now resolve existing filesystem path segments before checking that the destination remains inside that directory. Downloads through a symbolic link that resolves outside the configured directory are rejected. Valid object names using `/` as a pseudo-directory separator continue to work. | あり | Local downloads configured with a plain `downloadFileName` directory now resolve existing filesystem<br>path segments before checking that the destination remains inside that directory. Downloads through a<br>symbolic link that resolves outside the configured directory are rejected. Valid object names using `/`<br>as a pseudo-directory separator continue to work. | 未調査 |  |
| C115 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-spring-redis | camel-spring-redis - the default serializer applies a deserialization filter | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-spring-redis, JdkSerializationRedisSerializer, java.io.ObjectInputFilter, jdk.serialFilter, java.net.**, deserializationFilter, serializer, RedisSerializer | The default serializer, `JdkSerializationRedisSerializer`, now installs a `java.io.ObjectInputFilter` while reading Redis payloads. Previously no filter was applied at all. This affects both the consumer, which deserializes the payload of every message published to the subscribed channels, and the producer read commands, which deserialize the values stored in Redis. When no explicit pattern is configured, the JVM-wide `jdk.serialFilter` is honoured if set, otherwise a conservative default allow- | あり | The default serializer, `JdkSerializationRedisSerializer`, now installs a<br>`java.io.ObjectInputFilter` while reading Redis payloads. Previously no filter was applied at all.<br>This affects both the consumer, which deserializes the payload of every message published to the<br>subscribed channels, and the producer read commands, which deserialize the values stored in Redis.<br><br>When no explicit pattern is configured, the JVM-wide `jdk.serialFilter` is honoured if set,<br>otherwise a conservative default allow-list is applied (it permits standard Java and Apache Camel<br>types and denies `java.net.**`). Routes that exchange classes outside that allow-list must widen it<br>through the new `deserializationFilter` endpoint option, for example:<br><br>[source,java]<br>[code]<br>    from("spring-redis://localhost:6379?command=SUBSCRIBE&channels=myChannel"<br>         + "&deserializationFilter=com.example.model.**;java.**;!*")<br><br>Setting the `serializer` option to a custom `RedisSerializer` bypasses the filter entirely, since<br>Camel then no longer controls how the payload is read. | 未調査 |  |
| C116 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-google-mail | camel-google-mail - the raw option now returns the message | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-google-mail, google-mail-stream, FULL, raw, RAW, raw=true, null, payload, CamelGoogleMailStreamSubject, ...From, ...To, ...Cc, ...Bcc, ...MessageId | The `google-mail-stream` consumer always asked the Gmail API for the `FULL` message format, but the `raw` field is only populated for the `RAW` format, so `raw=true` produced a `null` body. The consumer now requests the format that matches the option. Because the Gmail API does not return the parsed `payload` for the `RAW` format, a route running with `raw=true` no longer receives the `CamelGoogleMailStreamSubject`, `...From`, `...To`, `...Cc`, `...Bcc` and `...MessageId` headers — those values | あり | The `google-mail-stream` consumer always asked the Gmail API for the `FULL` message format, but the<br>`raw` field is only populated for the `RAW` format, so `raw=true` produced a `null` body. The consumer<br>now requests the format that matches the option.<br><br>Because the Gmail API does not return the parsed `payload` for the `RAW` format, a route running with<br>`raw=true` no longer receives the `CamelGoogleMailStreamSubject`, `...From`, `...To`, `...Cc`, `...Bcc`<br>and `...MessageId` headers — those values are part of the RFC 2822 content that is now in the body.<br>`CamelGoogleMailStreamId`, `...ThreadId` and `...LabelIds` are still set. Routes that need the parsed<br>headers should keep the default `raw=false`. | 未調査 |  |
| C117 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-google-vertexai | camel-google-vertexai - the jsonMode option is now applied | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-google-vertexai, jsonMode=true, application/json, false | The option was declared and documented but never read, so it had no effect. `jsonMode=true` now sets the response MIME type of the request to `application/json`, so the model is asked to answer with JSON. The default `false` leaves the request untouched. |  | The option was declared and documented but never read, so it had no effect. `jsonMode=true` now sets<br>the response MIME type of the request to `application/json`, so the model is asked to answer with<br>JSON. The default `false` leaves the request untouched. | 未調査 |  |
| C118 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 高 | 削除 | camel-infinispan | camel-infinispan - the aggregation repository keeps completed exchanges for recovery | pom/gradle の依存、URIスキーム(例: xxx:)、import、DSL要素を grep。代替先が記載されていれば置換計画を立てる | camel-infinispan, InfinispanAggregationRepository, RecoverableAggregationRepository, remove, confirm, scan, CamelRedelivered, maximumRedeliveries, camel-recovery:<exchange id>, recover, getKeys, useRecovery=false | `InfinispanAggregationRepository` implements `RecoverableAggregationRepository`, but it had no recovery store: `remove` deleted the completed exchange outright, `confirm` removed the exchange id from a cache keyed by correlation key, and `scan` returned the correlation keys of the aggregations still in progress. The recovery task therefore re-delivered aggregations that were still accumulating, marked them `CamelRedelivered`, and sent them to the dead letter channel once `maximumRedeliveries` wa | あり | `InfinispanAggregationRepository` implements `RecoverableAggregationRepository`, but it had no recovery<br>store: `remove` deleted the completed exchange outright, `confirm` removed the exchange id from a cache<br>keyed by correlation key, and `scan` returned the correlation keys of the aggregations still in progress.<br>The recovery task therefore re-delivered aggregations that were still accumulating, marked them<br>`CamelRedelivered`, and sent them to the dead letter channel once `maximumRedeliveries` was reached, while<br>exchanges that genuinely failed after completion could never be recovered.<br><br>A completed exchange is now kept in the same cache under a `camel-recovery:<exchange id>` key until it is<br>confirmed, which is what `scan` reports and `recover` reads. `getKeys` continues to report only the<br>aggregations in progress.<br><br>Routes that set `useRecovery=false` are unaffected. Routes that left recovery enabled, which is the<br>default, stop seeing in-progress aggregations re-delivered, and start seeing genuine recovery. The cache<br>now also holds one entry per completed and not yet confirmed exchange; those entries are removed on<br>confirmation. | 未調査 |  |
| C119 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 高 | ヘッダー名/フィルタ変更 | camel-vertx-http | camel-vertx-http - the REST producer applies the outbound header filter | ヘッダー名の文字列リテラル、Constants 参照、headerFilterStrategy 設定を grep（新旧値は「Camelヘッダー値変更」シート）。外部システムとの連携ヘッダーにも注意 | camel-vertx-http, VertxHttpRestHeaderFilterStrategy.applyFilterToCamelHeaders, applyFilterToExternalHeaders, VertxHttpHeaderFilterStrategy, camel-http-common, camel-netty-http, camel-undertow, vertx-http, headerFilterStrategy, Content-Length, Content-Type, Host, Cache-Control, Connection | `VertxHttpRestHeaderFilterStrategy.applyFilterToCamelHeaders` delegated to `applyFilterToExternalHeaders`, so it consulted the inbound filter while implementing the outbound direction. The outbound filter that `VertxHttpHeaderFilterStrategy` installs was therefore never applied, and the sibling REST strategies in `camel-http-common`, `camel-netty-http` and `camel-undertow` all delegate to the matching method. A REST producer that resolves to `vertx-http` and does not configure its own `headerFil | あり | `VertxHttpRestHeaderFilterStrategy.applyFilterToCamelHeaders` delegated to<br>`applyFilterToExternalHeaders`, so it consulted the inbound filter while implementing the outbound<br>direction. The outbound filter that `VertxHttpHeaderFilterStrategy` installs was therefore never<br>applied, and the sibling REST strategies in `camel-http-common`, `camel-netty-http` and<br>`camel-undertow` all delegate to the matching method.<br><br>A REST producer that resolves to `vertx-http` and does not configure its own `headerFilterStrategy`<br>now filters the common HTTP headers on the outbound direction, as the other HTTP components already<br>did. A message header named `Content-Length`, `Content-Type`, `Host`, `Cache-Control`, `Connection`,<br>`Date`, `Pragma`, `Trailer`, `Transfer-Encoding`, `Upgrade`, `Via` or `Warning` is no longer copied<br>onto the outgoing request; the `Content-Type` of the request is still taken from the exchange as<br>before. Headers consumed by the URI template or the query parameters continue to be filtered, and a<br>custom `headerFilterStrategy` is used as-is and is unaffected.<br><br>Routes that relied on one of those headers reaching the wire must set it through the endpoint<br>configuration or supply a `headerFilterStrategy` that permits it. | 未調査 |  |
| C120 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-jgroups | camel-jgroups - the consumer applies a deserialization filter by default | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-jgroups, org.jgroups.Message.getObject(), java.io.ObjectInputFilter, jdk.serialFilter, deserializationFilter, deserializationFilter=*, ExceptionHandler, AUTH | The consumer maps an incoming cluster message to the exchange body by calling `org.jgroups.Message.getObject()`, which Java-deserializes the payload. The class of that body is now checked against a JEP-290 `java.io.ObjectInputFilter`. Previously no filter was applied at all, and any type a cluster peer sent was routed. When no explicit pattern is configured, the JVM-wide `jdk.serialFilter` is honoured if set, otherwise a conservative default allow-list is applied (`!java.net.**;java.**;javax.**; | あり | The consumer maps an incoming cluster message to the exchange body by calling<br>`org.jgroups.Message.getObject()`, which Java-deserializes the payload. The class of that body is<br>now checked against a JEP-290 `java.io.ObjectInputFilter`. Previously no filter was applied at all,<br>and any type a cluster peer sent was routed.<br><br>When no explicit pattern is configured, the JVM-wide `jdk.serialFilter` is honoured if set,<br>otherwise a conservative default allow-list is applied<br>(`+!java.net.**;java.**;javax.**;org.apache.camel.**;!*+`), matching the default the other components<br>that can receive Java-serialized payloads have applied since 4.18.3. Routes that exchange their own<br>classes over the cluster must widen it through the new `deserializationFilter` endpoint option, for<br>example:<br><br>[source,java]<br>[code]<br>    from("jgroups:clusterName?deserializationFilter=com.example.model.**;java.**;!*")<br><br>Setting `deserializationFilter=*` accepts any type and so restores the previous behavior. A refused<br>message is not routed; it is reported to the consumer's `ExceptionHandler`, which logs it at WARN<br>level by default.<br><br>Note that JGroups deserializes the payload inside its own receive path, so this check is a<br>defense-in-depth allow-list on the resulting body type. The JVM-wide `jdk.serialFilter`, together<br>with a channel secured with `AUTH` and encryption, remain the primary mitigations. | 未調査 |  |
| C121 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-smooks | camel-smooks - external XML entity resolution disabled by default | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-smooks, allowExternalEntities, true | The Smooks data format and the Smooks component now parse XML input with a reader that does not resolve external general or parameter entities, aligning camel-smooks with the XML parser configuration already applied by the other Camel XML components. When a Smooks configuration parses XML with the default reader, an untrusted body can no longer pull in external entities (for example `<!ENTITY x SYSTEM "file:///...">` or an `http://` reference). This only affects configurations that use the defau | あり | The Smooks data format and the Smooks component now parse XML input with a reader that does not<br>resolve external general or parameter entities, aligning camel-smooks with the XML parser<br>configuration already applied by the other Camel XML components. When a Smooks configuration parses<br>XML with the default reader, an untrusted body can no longer pull in external entities (for example<br>`<!ENTITY x SYSTEM "file:///...">` or an `http://` reference).<br><br>This only affects configurations that use the default XML reader. Configurations that declare their<br>own reader — EDI, CSV, JSON, DFDL, or a custom reader — are unchanged, and marshalling is unchanged.<br>Documents carrying an internal DTD subset still parse.<br><br>To restore the previous behaviour and allow external entity resolution, set the new<br>`allowExternalEntities` option to `true` on the data format or on the endpoint<br>(`smooks:config.xml?allowExternalEntities=true`). | 未調査 |  |
| C122 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 高 | 破壊的変更(要確認) | camel-mustache, camel-chunk | camel-mustache, camel-chunk - potential breaking change | 原文全文を読み、該当するオプション/クラス/ヘッダー/URI を grep。使用していれば修正が必要 | camel-mustache, camel-chunk, MustacheConstants, ChunkConstants, DefaultHeaderFilterStrategy, MustacheConstants.MUSTACHE_RESOURCE_URI, MustacheResourceUri, CamelMustacheResourceUri, MustacheConstants.MUSTACHE_TEMPLATE, MustacheTemplate, CamelMustacheTemplate, ChunkConstants.CHUNK_RESOURCE_URI, ChunkResourceUri, CamelChunkResourceUri | The Exchange header constants in `MustacheConstants` and `ChunkConstants` have been renamed to follow the Camel naming convention used across the rest of the component catalog (so that they are handled consistently by `DefaultHeaderFilterStrategy`, like the other template components). The Java field names are unchanged; only the header string values have changed. `MustacheConstants`: \| Constant \| Previous value \| New value \| `MustacheConstants.MUSTACHE_RESOURCE_URI` \| `MustacheResourceUri` \| `Ca | あり | The Exchange header constants in `MustacheConstants` and `ChunkConstants` have been renamed to<br>follow the Camel naming convention used across the rest of the component catalog (so that they are<br>handled consistently by `DefaultHeaderFilterStrategy`, like the other template components). The Java<br>field names are unchanged; only the header string values have changed.<br><br>`MustacheConstants`:<br><br>[options="header"]<br>\|===<br>\| Constant \| Previous value \| New value<br>\| `MustacheConstants.MUSTACHE_RESOURCE_URI` \| `MustacheResourceUri` \| `CamelMustacheResourceUri`<br>\| `MustacheConstants.MUSTACHE_TEMPLATE` \| `MustacheTemplate` \| `CamelMustacheTemplate`<br>\|===<br><br>`ChunkConstants`:<br><br>[options="header"]<br>\|===<br>\| Constant \| Previous value \| New value<br>\| `ChunkConstants.CHUNK_RESOURCE_URI` \| `ChunkResourceUri` \| `CamelChunkResourceUri`<br>\| `ChunkConstants.CHUNK_TEMPLATE` \| `ChunkTemplate` \| `CamelChunkTemplate`<br>\|===<br><br>Both components read these headers only when `allowTemplateFromHeader=true`. Because the old names sat<br>outside the `Camel` namespace, `DefaultHeaderFilterStrategy` did not strip them, so such a header<br>arriving from an untrusted sender reached the producer; with the `Camel` prefix the headers are now<br>filtered at the component boundary, so the template or resource can only come from the route. For that<br>reason the old names should not simply be restored.<br><br>Routes that reference the constants (for example `setHeader(MustacheConstants.MUSTACHE_TEMPLATE, ...)`)<br>are unaffected. Routes that set the header by its literal string name, or that use<br>`allowTemplateFromHeader=true` with the old header names, must switch to the new `Camel`-prefixed<br>names. | 未調査 |  |
| C123 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-saga | camel-saga | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-saga, saga:complete, saga:compensate, Long-Running-Action, CamelSagaService, true, isLongRunningActionHeaderSupported(), LRASagaService, InMemorySagaService, IllegalStateException, exchange.getExchangeExtension().setSagaLongRunningAction(id) | The `saga:complete` and `saga:compensate` endpoints now follow the same rule as the Saga EIP: when the exchange is not bound to a saga, the `Long-Running-Action` message header is consulted only if the configured `CamelSagaService` returns `true` from `isLongRunningActionHeaderSupported()`, as `LRASagaService` does. With the default `InMemorySagaService` the header is ignored, and sending an exchange that is not bound to a saga to these endpoints fails with `IllegalStateException`. Exchanges cre | あり | The `saga:complete` and `saga:compensate` endpoints now follow the same rule as the Saga EIP: when the<br>exchange is not bound to a saga, the `Long-Running-Action` message header is consulted only if the<br>configured `CamelSagaService` returns `true` from `isLongRunningActionHeaderSupported()`, as<br>`LRASagaService` does. With the default `InMemorySagaService` the header is ignored, and sending an<br>exchange that is not bound to a saga to these endpoints fails with `IllegalStateException`.<br><br>Exchanges created inside a saga, including copies made by EIPs such as Wire Tap, Multicast or SEDA,<br>stay bound to it and are not affected. A route that completes or compensates an in-memory saga from an<br>unrelated exchange must bind it explicitly from trusted code, for example with<br>`exchange.getExchangeExtension().setSagaLongRunningAction(id)`. | 未調査 |  |
| C124 | 4.18.4→4.18.5 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-coap | camel-coap | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-coap, 5.00 Internal Server Error, 2.05 Content, byte[], muteException, true, camel-knative, camel-mina, camel-cxf, camel-grpc, restConfiguration().endpointProperty("muteException", "false") | The CoAP consumer now answers a failed exchange with `5.00 Internal Server Error`, and no longer returns the exception's message to the client. Previously the consumer did not check whether the exchange had failed: a route failure was answered with `2.05 Content` and whatever body the message held when the route failed. When the response could not be built once the route had completed, for example because the body could not be converted to `byte[]`, the exception's message was sent to the client | あり | The CoAP consumer now answers a failed exchange with `5.00 Internal Server Error`, and no longer returns<br>the exception's message to the client.<br><br>Previously the consumer did not check whether the exchange had failed: a route failure was answered with<br>`2.05 Content` and whatever body the message held when the route failed. When the response could not be<br>built once the route had completed, for example because the body could not be converted to `byte[]`, the<br>exception's message was sent to the client as the payload of a `5.00 Internal Server Error` response.<br><br>A new `muteException` consumer option controls the payload of the error response, and it defaults to<br>`true`, the same default as the HTTP consumers and the `camel-knative`, `camel-mina`, `camel-cxf` and<br>`camel-grpc` consumers: the payload is empty. A route that relies on the exception's message reaching the<br>client must opt back in explicitly:<br><br>[source,text]<br>[code]<br>    coap://0.0.0.0:5683/my/resource?muteException=false<br><br>For the Rest DSL, set it with `restConfiguration().endpointProperty("muteException", "false")`. | 未調査 |  |
| C125 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-lra | camel-lra - only an LRA hosted by the configured coordinator is joined | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-lra, coordinatorUrl, coordinatorContextPath, Long-Running-Action, allowedCoordinatorUrls, camel.lra.allowedCoordinatorUrls | The LRA saga service now joins, completes or compensates an LRA only when its URL is under the configured LRA coordinator (`coordinatorUrl` followed by `coordinatorContextPath`). A message whose `Long-Running-Action` header holds the URL of another coordinator now fails, instead of the saga service calling that URL. If your participants reach the same coordinator under different URLs, for example with different host names, list the other coordinator URLs, including the context path, in the new ` | あり | The LRA saga service now joins, completes or compensates an LRA only when its URL is under the configured LRA<br>coordinator (`coordinatorUrl` followed by `coordinatorContextPath`). A message whose `Long-Running-Action` header holds<br>the URL of another coordinator now fails, instead of the saga service calling that URL.<br><br>If your participants reach the same coordinator under different URLs, for example with different host names, list the<br>other coordinator URLs, including the context path, in the new `allowedCoordinatorUrls` option<br>(`camel.lra.allowedCoordinatorUrls`).<br><br>This also applies to the LRAs the service starts itself, which a nested saga route looks up again: when the<br>coordinator returns LRA URLs under another base URL than `coordinatorUrl`, for example behind a proxy, add that<br>base URL to `allowedCoordinatorUrls` as well. | 未調査 |  |
| C126 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-lra | camel-lra - warning for unsigned participant callbacks, and reserved saga option names | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-lra, callbackSecret, Camel-Saga-Compensate, Camel-Saga-Complete, Camel-Saga-Signature, Camel-Saga-Lra | The LRA saga service now logs a warning at startup when no `callbackSecret` is configured: the callbacks that the LRA coordinator sends to the participant endpoints are then not verified, so these endpoints must only be reachable by the coordinator. The Saga EIP documentation describes how to sign them. A saga option named like a parameter of the participant callback URLs, in any case (`Camel-Saga-Compensate`, `Camel-Saga-Complete`, `Camel-Saga-Signature` or `Camel-Saga-Lra`), now fails the crea | あり | The LRA saga service now logs a warning at startup when no `callbackSecret` is configured: the callbacks that the LRA<br>coordinator sends to the participant endpoints are then not verified, so these endpoints must only be reachable by the<br>coordinator. The Saga EIP documentation describes how to sign them.<br><br>A saga option named like a parameter of the participant callback URLs, in any case (`Camel-Saga-Compensate`,<br>`Camel-Saga-Complete`, `Camel-Saga-Signature` or `Camel-Saga-Lra`), now fails the creation of its route, with or<br>without a callback secret. The value of such an option could not be read back from the callback before. | 未調査 |  |
| C127 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-ssh | camel-ssh - known_hosts entries are matched as in OpenSSH | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-ssh, knownHostsResource, known_hosts, host, [host]:port, db.example.com, [db.example.com]:2222, HostName, ::1, @revoked, @cert-authority, failOnUnknownHost | When `knownHostsResource` is set, the entries of the `known_hosts` file are now matched as OpenSSH matches them, against the host name or address the client connects to, written `host` for port 22 and `[host]:port` for another port: * A host pattern must match that name as a whole. An entry for another host whose name or address contains the configured one is no longer used. * A pattern such as `db.example.com` only applies to port 22. A server on another port needs an entry such as `[db.example | あり | When `knownHostsResource` is set, the entries of the `known_hosts` file are now matched as OpenSSH<br>matches them, against the host name or address the client connects to, written `host` for port 22 and<br>`[host]:port` for another port:<br><br>* A host pattern must match that name as a whole. An entry for another host whose name or address<br>contains the configured one is no longer used.<br>* A pattern such as `db.example.com` only applies to port 22. A server on another port needs an entry<br>such as `[db.example.com]:2222`, or a pattern that matches that form, such as `*`.<br>* The host is used as it is written: the host configured on the endpoint, or the `HostName` that<br>`~/.ssh/config` gives for it. An endpoint configured with a host name no longer uses entries listed<br>only under its address, an endpoint configured with an address no longer uses entries listed under the<br>name that a reverse lookup of the address returns, and an IPv6 address such as `::1` is matched as<br>written instead of in its expanded form.<br>* Every entry of the host is considered, so a host can be listed with several keys. Wildcard, negated<br>and hashed patterns, comments and `@revoked` entries are supported. `@cert-authority` entries are<br>ignored.<br>* A `known_hosts` resource that cannot be read, or that has a line that cannot be read (for example an<br>unknown `@` marker, a missing key, or a key that is not valid base64), now makes every connection fail,<br>whatever the value of `failOnUnknownHost`. Previously such a resource was treated as having no entry,<br>and such a line was skipped.<br><br>As before, a server whose host has entries without a marker is rejected when it presents a key that<br>none of them lists, whatever the value of `failOnUnknownHost`, which only applies to a host without<br>such an entry: `@revoked` and `@cert-authority` entries do not list a host.<br>Check that the `known_hosts` file has an entry for the host name or address, and the port, that each<br>endpoint is configured with. | 未調査 |  |
| C128 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-netty-http | camel-netty-http - the roles of a security constraint are matched by role name | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-netty-http, SecurityConstraintMapping, admin guest, admin,guest | The roles of a `SecurityConstraintMapping` inclusion are a comma-separated list of role names, and an authenticated user is now accepted only when one of their roles is equal to one of those names. The comparison is case-sensitive, whitespace around each name and blank entries are ignored, and a value of `*` still accepts any role. A roles value that uses another separator, such as `admin;guest` or `admin guest`, must be changed to `admin,guest`. | あり | The roles of a `SecurityConstraintMapping` inclusion are a comma-separated list of role names, and an authenticated<br>user is now accepted only when one of their roles is equal to one of those names. The comparison is case-sensitive,<br>whitespace around each name and blank entries are ignored, and a value of `*` still accepts any role. A roles value<br>that uses another separator, such as `admin;guest` or `admin guest`, must be changed to `admin,guest`. | 未調査 |  |
| C129 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-graphql | camel-graphql - the credentials are only sent to the authority of httpUri | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-graphql, accessToken, jwtAuthorizationType, username, password, proxyHost | The `accessToken` (with its `jwtAuthorizationType`) is now added to a request only when the request goes to the scheme, host and port of `httpUri`, and the `username` and `password` are registered for that authority only. Previously neither was bound to the authority of `httpUri`. A request to another authority, including the same host with another scheme or port, is now sent without them. A route that needs to authenticate to another authority must address it in `httpUri`. When `proxyHost` is s | あり | The `accessToken` (with its `jwtAuthorizationType`) is now added to a request only when the request goes to the<br>scheme, host and port of `httpUri`, and the `username` and `password` are registered for that authority only.<br>Previously neither was bound to the authority of `httpUri`. A request to another authority, including the same host<br>with another scheme or port, is now sent without them. A route that needs to authenticate to another authority must<br>address it in `httpUri`. When `proxyHost` is set, the `username` and `password` are no longer offered to the proxy when<br>it asks for authentication. | 未調査 |  |
| C130 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-elasticsearch | camel-elasticsearch - the credentials are only sent to the nodes of the cluster | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-elasticsearch, user, password, hostAddresses, enableSniffer, client | The `user` and `password` are now offered only to the nodes the client talks to: the hosts of `hostAddresses` and, when `enableSniffer` is set, the nodes the sniffer finds, matching the scheme, host and port. Previously they were registered for any host. A request to another host, including the host of a node with another scheme or port, is now sent without them. A client set with the `client` option is not changed. | あり | The `user` and `password` are now offered only to the nodes the client talks to: the hosts of `hostAddresses` and,<br>when `enableSniffer` is set, the nodes the sniffer finds, matching the scheme, host and port. Previously they were<br>registered for any host. A request to another host, including the host of a node with another scheme or port, is now<br>sent without them. A client set with the `client` option is not changed. | 未調査 |  |
| C131 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-elasticsearch-rest-client | camel-elasticsearch-rest-client - the credentials are only sent to the nodes of the cluster | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-elasticsearch-rest-client, user, password, hostAddressesList, enableSniffer, restClient | The `user` and `password` are now offered only to the nodes the client talks to: the hosts of `hostAddressesList` and, when `enableSniffer` is set, the nodes the sniffer finds, matching the scheme, host and port. Previously they were registered for any host. A request to another host, including the host of a node with another scheme or port, is now sent without them. A client set with the `restClient` option is not changed. | あり | The `user` and `password` are now offered only to the nodes the client talks to: the hosts of `hostAddressesList` and,<br>when `enableSniffer` is set, the nodes the sniffer finds, matching the scheme, host and port. Previously they were<br>registered for any host. A request to another host, including the host of a node with another scheme or port, is now<br>sent without them. A client set with the `restClient` option is not changed. | 未調査 |  |
| C132 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-opensearch | camel-opensearch - the credentials are only sent to the nodes of the cluster | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-opensearch, user, password, hostAddresses, enableSniffer, client | The `user` and `password` are now offered only to the nodes the client talks to: the hosts of `hostAddresses` and, when `enableSniffer` is set, the nodes the sniffer finds, matching the scheme, host and port. Every node of a multi-host cluster still receives them. Previously they were registered for any host. A request to another host, including the host of a node with another scheme or port, is now sent without them. A client set with the `client` option is not changed. | あり | The `user` and `password` are now offered only to the nodes the client talks to: the hosts of `hostAddresses` and,<br>when `enableSniffer` is set, the nodes the sniffer finds, matching the scheme, host and port. Every node of a<br>multi-host cluster still receives them. Previously they were registered for any host. A request to another host,<br>including the host of a node with another scheme or port, is now sent without them. A client set with the `client`<br>option is not changed. | 未調査 |  |
| C133 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-telegram | camel-telegram - webhook requests are verified with a secret token | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-telegram, X-Telegram-Bot-Api-Secret-Token, webhookSecretToken, webhookAutoRegister=true, WebhookRoutePolicy, secret_token, setWebhook, webhookPath, webhookSecretTokenEnabled=false | In webhook mode, Camel now registers the webhook with a secret token, which Telegram sends in the `X-Telegram-Bot-Api-Secret-Token` header of every webhook request, and rejects a webhook request that does not carry the token with HTTP 403. The token is the value of the new `webhookSecretToken` option or, when that option is not set, a value derived from the authorization token. A client that calls the webhook endpoint directly, for example in a test, must send the header too. When Camel register | あり | In webhook mode, Camel now registers the webhook with a secret token, which Telegram sends in the<br>`X-Telegram-Bot-Api-Secret-Token` header of every webhook request, and rejects a webhook request that does not carry<br>the token with HTTP 403. The token is the value of the new `webhookSecretToken` option or, when that option is not<br>set, a value derived from the authorization token. A client that calls the webhook endpoint directly, for example in<br>a test, must send the header too.<br><br>When Camel registers the webhook at startup (`webhookAutoRegister=true`, the default), nothing needs to change: each<br>upgraded instance registers the webhook with the token, and the instances that are not upgraded yet ignore the header.<br><br>When the webhook is registered in another way, through the `WebhookRoutePolicy` or outside Camel, the registration<br>made with the previous version has no secret token, and the upgraded instances reject every update until the webhook<br>is registered again with it. Register the webhook again first, then upgrade the instances that receive the updates:<br>instances of the previous version ignore the header. With the `WebhookRoutePolicy`, run the registration with the new<br>version. Outside Camel, set `webhookSecretToken`, and pass the same value as the `secret_token` parameter of<br>`setWebhook`.<br><br>The default webhook path is a hash of the endpoint URI, so adding an option to the URI, such as `webhookSecretToken`,<br>changes the path. When the webhook is not registered by Camel at startup, set `webhookPath` to keep the path that is<br>registered.<br><br>To restore the previous behaviour, set `webhookSecretTokenEnabled=false`: the webhook is then registered without a<br>secret token, and webhook requests are not verified. | 未調査 |  |
| C134 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-thrift | camel-thrift - SSL/TLS settings of the consumer, and errors sent to the client | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-thrift, negotiationType=SSL, synchronous=false, synchronous=true, SASL, PLAINTEXT, sslParameters, serverParameters.clientAuthentication=REQUIRE, keyPassword, muteException, TApplicationException, INTERNAL_ERROR, muteException=false | * A consumer configured with `negotiationType=SSL` and the default `synchronous=false` now fails to start. It used to start a server without SSL/TLS. SSL/TLS requires `synchronous=true`, as on the producer. * The `SASL` negotiation type, which is not implemented, now makes the producer and the consumer fail to start. They used to behave as with `PLAINTEXT`. * The SSL/TLS consumer now creates its server socket from the SSL context of `sslParameters`, so all of its settings apply, including the tr | あり | * A consumer configured with `negotiationType=SSL` and the default `synchronous=false` now fails to start. It used to<br>start a server without SSL/TLS. SSL/TLS requires `synchronous=true`, as on the producer.<br>* The `SASL` negotiation type, which is not implemented, now makes the producer and the consumer fail to start. They<br>used to behave as with `PLAINTEXT`.<br>* The SSL/TLS consumer now creates its server socket from the SSL context of `sslParameters`, so all of its settings<br>apply, including the trust managers and the server parameters. For example, `serverParameters.clientAuthentication=REQUIRE`<br>now requires a client certificate. Before, the consumer only used the key store, the protocol and the cipher suites,<br>and never asked for a client certificate. The key managers are required, and as in the other components the key is<br>read with their `keyPassword`: set it when the key is protected by a password. Before, the consumer read the key with<br>the password of the key store.<br>* A new `muteException` consumer option, enabled by default, sends a generic `TApplicationException` of type<br>`INTERNAL_ERROR` ("Internal error processing <method>") to the client instead of an exception that the Thrift method<br>does not declare. Before, the asynchronous consumer sent the message of the exception. An exception declared in the<br>IDL is still sent as it is. Set `muteException=false` to send the exception as before. | 未調査 |  |
| C135 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-oauth | camel-oauth - the bearer token processor is stateless and reads the HTTP request headers | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-oauth, OAuthBearerTokenProcessor, camel.oauth.session, CamelOAuthSessionId, UserProfile, CamelOAuthUserProfile, OAuth.CAMEL_OAUTH_USER_PROFILE, Authorization, Cookie, authenticate(new TokenCredentials(token)), OAuth, OAuthFactory.lookupFactory(context).findOAuth() | `OAuthBearerTokenProcessor` no longer creates or updates the OAuth session, and no longer sets the `camel.oauth.session` cookie nor the `CamelOAuthSessionId` header: a bearer token authenticates the request that carries it, and nothing else. The authenticated `UserProfile` is available in the `CamelOAuthUserProfile` exchange property (`OAuth.CAMEL_OAUTH_USER_PROFILE`). A route that read the profile from the OAuth session after `OAuthBearerTokenProcessor` must read it from that exchange property | あり | `OAuthBearerTokenProcessor` no longer creates or updates the OAuth session, and no longer sets the<br>`camel.oauth.session` cookie nor the `CamelOAuthSessionId` header: a bearer token authenticates the request that<br>carries it, and nothing else. The authenticated `UserProfile` is available in the `CamelOAuthUserProfile` exchange<br>property (`OAuth.CAMEL_OAUTH_USER_PROFILE`). A route that read the profile from the OAuth session after<br>`OAuthBearerTokenProcessor` must read it from that exchange property instead.<br><br>When the message comes from platform-http (on Vert.x or on Spring Boot), from a servlet based HTTP consumer<br>(camel-servlet, camel-jetty) or from camel-netty-http, the bearer token and the session cookie are now read from the<br>`Authorization` and `Cookie` headers of the HTTP request only. A query or form parameter of the same name, which these<br>consumers also map into the message headers, is no longer used for them, and neither is an `Authorization` or `Cookie`<br>message header that the route sets itself. For example<br>`+.setHeader("Authorization", simple("Bearer ${header.X-Forwarded-Access-Token}"))+` before<br>`OAuthBearerTokenProcessor` now results in a 401 response. A route that takes the token from elsewhere can<br>authenticate it itself, with `authenticate(new TokenCredentials(token))` on the `OAuth` instance that<br>`OAuthFactory.lookupFactory(context).findOAuth()` returns.<br><br>For any other message, such as one from camel-undertow, the processors keep reading the message headers, but leave out<br>a value that also appears in the query string of the request. | 未調査 |  |
| C136 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-smooks | camel-smooks - allowExternalEntities applies to Smooks Source bodies and to a reader declared without a class | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-smooks, SmooksProcessor, Source, org.smooks.io.source.StringSource, ReaderSource, StreamSource, ByteSource, URLSource, allowExternalEntities, true, DOMSource, JavaSource, <reader>, class | The Smooks component and `SmooksProcessor` now parse a message body that is already a Smooks `Source` (`org.smooks.io.source.StringSource`, `ReaderSource`, `StreamSource`, `ByteSource` or `URLSource`) with the same XML reader settings as the other message bodies, so external entities in it are only resolved when `allowExternalEntities` is `true`. Before, such a body was handed to Smooks as it was. The bytes of a `StreamSource`, `ByteSource` or `URLSource` are decoded with the exchange charset, o | あり | The Smooks component and `SmooksProcessor` now parse a message body that is already a Smooks `Source`<br>(`org.smooks.io.source.StringSource`, `ReaderSource`, `StreamSource`, `ByteSource` or `URLSource`) with the same XML<br>reader settings as the other message bodies, so external entities in it are only resolved when `allowExternalEntities`<br>is `true`. Before, such a body was handed to Smooks as it was. The bytes of a `StreamSource`, `ByteSource` or<br>`URLSource` are decoded with the exchange charset, or UTF-8, as before. These bodies are now parsed into a DOM before<br>Smooks processes them, which uses more memory for very large inputs. `DOMSource` and `JavaSource` bodies are unchanged.<br><br>A Smooks configuration whose `<reader>` declares no `class` only sets handlers, features or parameters of the default<br>XML reader. The Smooks component, `SmooksProcessor` and the Smooks data format now apply `allowExternalEntities` to<br>such a configuration as they do to a configuration without a `<reader>`, so the handlers and features of that reader no<br>longer take part in parsing the XML unless `allowExternalEntities` is `true`. Configurations that declare a reader<br>class, such as EDI, CSV, JSON, DFDL or a custom reader, are unchanged.<br><br>A document with a document type declaration (`<!DOCTYPE ...>`) is now processed with these settings for every body<br>type, `String`, `byte[]` and `InputStream` bodies included, which failed before: the entities of its internal subset are<br>expanded, and the declaration itself is not passed on to Smooks.<br><br>Set `allowExternalEntities=true` on the endpoint or on the data format for documents that rely on external entities or<br>an external DTD. | 未調査 |  |
| C137 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-chunk | camel-chunk - message data printed with a direct tag is rendered literally | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-chunk, chunk, {$headers.name}, {$tag}, slice, get, join, List, Collection, Set, {$body}, toString(), {$body.name}, {$exception.message} | Previously the `chunk` component bound the message body, headers and variables into the Chunk engine so that Chunk re-parsed those values as template source: a value that itself contained Chunk tags (such as a body of `{$headers.name}`) had those tags expanded instead of being printed verbatim. Message data bound to the engine is now neutralised so that a direct `{$tag}` printing the body, a header, a variable, or an element of a nested map, list or array renders the value literally instead of e | あり | Previously the `chunk` component bound the message body, headers and variables into the Chunk engine so that<br>Chunk re-parsed those values as template source: a value that itself contained Chunk tags (such as a body of<br>`{$headers.name}`) had those tags expanded instead of being printed verbatim.<br><br>Message data bound to the engine is now neutralised so that a direct `{$tag}` printing the body, a header, a<br>variable, or an element of a nested map, list or array renders the value literally instead of evaluating the<br>tags inside it. Tag-free data is unchanged and Chunk's string filters (for example `slice`, `get`, `join`)<br>keep their existing behaviour. Routes that relied on message data being evaluated as Chunk template code must<br>move that markup into the template itself.<br><br>As part of this, a non-`List` `Collection` value (such as a `Set`) is now bound as a `List`, so a direct<br>`{$body}` prints Chunk's list placeholder instead of the collection's `toString()`, and a filter such as<br>`\|join` operates on its elements.<br><br>This does not cover every path: Chunk re-parses any string it produces while rendering, so the output of a<br>filter (`{$body\|trim}`), a string property of a bean body (`{$body.name}`, `{$exception.message}`) and string<br>items bound by a `{% loop %}` are still parsed as template code. Keep untrusted data to direct `{$tag}`<br>output; do not pass untrusted values through Chunk filters, bean properties or loops. If untrusted data must<br>go through one of those paths, remove the `{` and `}` characters from it in the route before the `chunk`<br>endpoint (Chunk has no escape for tags inside data), for example with a processor that rewrites the body or<br>header. | 未調査 |  |
| C138 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 高 | ヘッダー名/フィルタ変更 | camel-spring-ws | camel-spring-ws - internal Camel headers are no longer written into SOAP headers | ヘッダー名の文字列リテラル、Constants 参照、headerFilterStrategy 設定を grep（新旧値は「Camelヘッダー値変更」シート）。外部システムとの連携ヘッダーにも注意 | camel-spring-ws, messageFilter, spring-ws, BasicMessageFilter, headerFilterStrategy, SpringWebserviceHeaderFilterStrategy, Camel, camel | The default `messageFilter` of the `spring-ws` component, `BasicMessageFilter`, now applies the endpoint's `headerFilterStrategy` before it writes a message header into the SOAP header, both on the request sent by the producer and on the response returned by the consumer. With the default `SpringWebserviceHeaderFilterStrategy`, message headers whose names start with `Camel` or `camel` (case-insensitively) are no longer written into the SOAP header. Other message headers are written as before. A | あり | The default `messageFilter` of the `spring-ws` component, `BasicMessageFilter`, now applies the endpoint's<br>`headerFilterStrategy` before it writes a message header into the SOAP header, both on the request sent by the<br>producer and on the response returned by the consumer. With the default `SpringWebserviceHeaderFilterStrategy`,<br>message headers whose names start with `Camel` or `camel` (case-insensitively) are no longer written into the SOAP<br>header. Other message headers are written as before.<br><br>A `BasicMessageFilter` created in application code applies a `SpringWebserviceHeaderFilterStrategy` unless it is<br>created with another strategy. A route that relies on the previous behaviour can supply a custom<br>`headerFilterStrategy` on the `spring-ws` endpoint. | 未調査 |  |
| C139 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-platform-http-main | camel-platform-http-main - management server settings on a shared port | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-platform-http-main, camel.server.enabled=true, camel.management.enabled=true, camel.management.host, camel.management.path, camel.management.useGlobalSslContextParameters=true, camel.management.authenticationEnabled=true, camel.management.port, /* | When the HTTP server (`camel.server.enabled=true`) and the HTTP management server (`camel.management.enabled=true`) have the same port (both default to 8080), the HTTP server serves the management endpoints. It cannot apply these options of the management server, which were ignored without notice: * `camel.management.host`, when set to another host than the one of the HTTP server * `camel.management.path`, when set to another path than the one of the HTTP server * `camel.management.useGlobalSslC | あり | When the HTTP server (`camel.server.enabled=true`) and the HTTP management server (`camel.management.enabled=true`)<br>have the same port (both default to 8080), the HTTP server serves the management endpoints. It cannot apply these<br>options of the management server, which were ignored without notice:<br><br>* `camel.management.host`, when set to another host than the one of the HTTP server<br>* `camel.management.path`, when set to another path than the one of the HTTP server<br>* `camel.management.useGlobalSslContextParameters=true`, when the HTTP server does not use the global SSL configuration<br>* `camel.management.authenticationEnabled=true`, with the basic or JWT authentication options<br><br>Camel now fails to start when the management server shares the port and one of them is configured, with a message<br>that lists them. Set `camel.management.port` to another port, so that the management server runs a server of its own<br>and applies them. To keep a single port, remove them and configure the HTTP server instead: for example,<br>authentication configured on the HTTP server for `/*` also applies to the management endpoints. | 未調査 |  |
| C140 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-mina | camel-mina - the host name of the server is verified over SSL | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-mina, sslContextParameters, useGlobalSslContextParameters, clientMode=true, host, hostnameVerification, false, writeTimeout, timeout, ERROR, org.apache.mina.filter.ssl.SslHandler | When `sslContextParameters` is set (on the endpoint, or with `useGlobalSslContextParameters` on the component), the producer, and the consumer with `clientMode=true`, now check that the certificate of the server matches the `host` of the endpoint, with the same rules as an HTTPS client. Before, any certificate trusted by the trust store was accepted, whatever host the certificate was issued for. A connection to a host name or an IP address that the certificate does not list now fails the SSL han | あり | When `sslContextParameters` is set (on the endpoint, or with `useGlobalSslContextParameters` on the component), the<br>producer, and the consumer with `clientMode=true`, now check that the certificate of the server matches the `host` of<br>the endpoint, with the same rules as an HTTPS client. Before, any certificate trusted by the trust store was accepted,<br>whatever host the certificate was issued for.<br><br>A connection to a host name or an IP address that the certificate does not list now fails the SSL handshake; an IP<br>address must be listed as a subject alternative name of the certificate. Use a certificate that matches the host of the<br>endpoint, or set the new `hostnameVerification` option to `false` to keep the previous behaviour.<br><br>MINA does not pass a failed handshake on as an error: the producer fails with a timeout (after `writeTimeout`, or<br>`timeout` while waiting for a reply), and the handshake error is logged at `ERROR` level by<br>`org.apache.mina.filter.ssl.SslHandler`. A producer that starts timing out after the upgrade should be checked for a<br>certificate that does not list the host. | 未調査 |  |
| C141 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-mllp | camel-mllp - the producer verifies the host name of the server over TLS | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-mllp, sslContextParameters, useGlobalSslContextParameters, mllp://8888, hostnameVerification, false | When `sslContextParameters` is set (on the endpoint, or with `useGlobalSslContextParameters` on the component), the MLLP producer now checks that the certificate of the server matches the host it connects to, with the same rules as an HTTPS client. Before, it accepted any certificate trusted by the trust store, whatever host the certificate was issued for. A producer that connects with a host name or an IP address that the certificate does not list now fails the TLS handshake; an IP address must | あり | When `sslContextParameters` is set (on the endpoint, or with `useGlobalSslContextParameters` on the component), the<br>MLLP producer now checks that the certificate of the server matches the host it connects to, with the same rules as an<br>HTTPS client. Before, it accepted any certificate trusted by the trust store, whatever host the certificate was issued<br>for.<br><br>A producer that connects with a host name or an IP address that the certificate does not list now fails the TLS<br>handshake; an IP address must be listed as a subject alternative name of the certificate, and a producer URI without a<br>host name (such as `mllp://8888`) cannot pass the check. Use a certificate that matches the host of the endpoint, or set<br>the new `hostnameVerification` option to `false` to keep the previous behaviour. | 未調査 |  |
| C142 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-smpp | camel-smpp - the host name of the SMSC is verified over SSL | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-smpp, smpps, usingSSL=true, hostnameVerification, false, IOException | With `smpps` (or `usingSSL=true`), directly or through an HTTP proxy, the connection now checks that the certificate of the SMSC matches the host it connects to, with the same rules as an HTTPS client. Before, any certificate trusted by the JVM was accepted, whatever host it was issued for. A connection to a host name or an IP address that the certificate does not list now fails the SSL handshake; an IP address must be listed as a subject alternative name of the certificate. Use a certificate th | あり | With `smpps` (or `usingSSL=true`), directly or through an HTTP proxy, the connection now checks that the certificate of<br>the SMSC matches the host it connects to, with the same rules as an HTTPS client. Before, any certificate trusted by the<br>JVM was accepted, whatever host it was issued for.<br><br>A connection to a host name or an IP address that the certificate does not list now fails the SSL handshake; an IP<br>address must be listed as a subject alternative name of the certificate. Use a certificate that matches the host of the<br>endpoint, or set the new `hostnameVerification` option to `false` to keep the previous behaviour.<br><br>A mismatch shows as the producer or consumer failing to start, with an `IOException` whose cause names the host that<br>was checked:<br><br>[source]<br>[code]<br>    java.io.IOException: No subject alternative names matching IP address 127.0.0.1 found<br>        Caused by: javax.net.ssl.SSLHandshakeException: No subject alternative names matching IP address 127.0.0.1 found | 未調査 |  |
| C143 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-milo | camel-milo - the OPC UA client validates the server certificate | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-milo, None, milo-client, milo-browse, trustListDirectory, trusted/certs, rejected/certs, Bad_CertificateRevocationUnknown, trusted/crl, issuer/crl, certificateValidator, WARN, IllegalStateException: No connected, Failed to connect, because a certificate or security check failed | When the endpoint it selects uses a security policy other than `None`, the `milo-client` and `milo-browse` components now validate the certificate of the OPC UA server. Before, any server certificate was accepted, and no option allowed to validate it. Configure the trusted certificates with the new `trustListDirectory` option: put the certificate of the server, or of the CA that issued it, in the `trusted/certs` directory of the trust list. A server certificate that does not chain up to a truste | あり | When the endpoint it selects uses a security policy other than `None`, the `milo-client` and `milo-browse` components<br>now validate the certificate of the OPC UA server. Before, any server certificate was accepted, and no option allowed<br>to validate it.<br><br>Configure the trusted certificates with the new `trustListDirectory` option: put the certificate of the server, or of<br>the CA that issued it, in the `trusted/certs` directory of the trust list. A server certificate that does not chain up<br>to a trusted certificate is written to `rejected/certs`, from where it can be moved to `trusted/certs` once it has been<br>checked. The trust list also refuses a server certificate outside its validity period, revoked by a revocation list of<br>the trust list, or whose application URI differs from the one of the server. A server certificate issued by a CA is<br>refused with `Bad_CertificateRevocationUnknown` when the trust list holds no revocation list of that CA: add the CRL of<br>the CA (an empty one is enough) to `trusted/crl` or `issuer/crl`. A custom validator can be configured with the new<br>`certificateValidator` option instead.<br><br>With neither option, the client does not connect to such an endpoint. It logs a `WARN` message<br>`Failed to connect: The certificate of the OPC UA server cannot be validated`, followed by the endpoint and its<br>security policy, and tries again every 10 seconds. A producer fails with `IllegalStateException: No connected`, caused<br>by the same message. A server certificate refused by the trust list or by a `certificateValidator` is logged at `WARN`<br>as `Failed to connect, because a certificate or security check failed`. To restore the previous behaviour, set the new<br>`trustAllCertificates` option to `true`. This is insecure: the client then does not authenticate the server.<br><br>The certificate that the user name and password are encrypted with is checked by the same validator. Endpoints that<br>use the security policy `None` are not affected. | 未調査 |  |
| C144 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-pqc | camel-pqc - Bouncy Castle 1.85 | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-pqc, DILITHIUM, SPHINCSPLUS, KYBER, BC, DilithiumParameterSpec, SPHINCSPlusParameterSpec, KyberParameterSpec, MLDSAParameterSpec, SLHDSAParameterSpec, MLKEMParameterSpec, org.bouncycastle.jcajce.spec, haraka_256s, slh_dsa_sha2_128s | Camel 4.18.6 upgrades Bouncy Castle from 1.83 to 1.85. `DILITHIUM`, `SPHINCSPLUS` and `KYBER` now use ML-DSA, SLH-DSA and ML-KEM through the `BC` provider. The Camel option names are unchanged. Replace `DilithiumParameterSpec`, `SPHINCSPlusParameterSpec` and `KyberParameterSpec` in user code with `MLDSAParameterSpec`, `SLHDSAParameterSpec` and `MLKEMParameterSpec` from `org.bouncycastle.jcajce.spec`, and use the `BC` provider when creating key material. Previously stored Dilithium and SPHINCS+ k |  | Camel 4.18.6 upgrades Bouncy Castle from 1.83 to 1.85. `DILITHIUM`, `SPHINCSPLUS` and `KYBER`<br>now use ML-DSA, SLH-DSA and ML-KEM through the `BC` provider. The Camel option names are unchanged.<br><br>Replace `DilithiumParameterSpec`, `SPHINCSPlusParameterSpec` and `KyberParameterSpec` in user code<br>with `MLDSAParameterSpec`, `SLHDSAParameterSpec` and `MLKEMParameterSpec` from<br>`org.bouncycastle.jcajce.spec`, and use the `BC` provider when creating key material.<br>Previously stored Dilithium and SPHINCS+ keys must be regenerated; update peers that use their public keys.<br><br>The default `SPHINCSPLUS` parameter set changes from `haraka_256s` (NIST category 5)<br>to `slh_dsa_sha2_128s` (category 1). Use an SLH-DSA 256-bit parameter set if category 5 is required. | 未調査 |  |
| C145 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-pqc | camel-pqc - a configured KeyGenerator is used by one exchange at a time | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-pqc, pqc, KeyGenerator, init, getKeyGenerator(), CamelPQCKemAlgorithm | When Camel creates the instance itself, the KEM operations of the `pqc` component and the PQC data format now use a new `KeyGenerator` for every operation. A configured instance is still shared: a registry bean, which is autowired into every `pqc` endpoint, a `KeyGenerator` set on the data format, or the default material used when no key material is configured. Each operation now holds its lock from `init` until the result is returned, so concurrent exchanges that use it run one at a time. A sta |  | When Camel creates the instance itself, the KEM operations of the `pqc` component and the PQC data format now use a<br>new `KeyGenerator` for every operation. A configured instance is still shared: a registry bean, which is autowired into<br>every `pqc` endpoint, a `KeyGenerator` set on the data format, or the default material used when no key material is<br>configured. Each operation now holds its lock from `init` until the result is returned, so concurrent exchanges that<br>use it run one at a time.<br><br>A started PQC data format no longer keeps a `KeyGenerator` of its own: `getKeyGenerator()` returns only a configured<br>instance and, unless one is configured, the `CamelPQCKemAlgorithm` header now selects the KEM algorithm of each<br>operation. | 未調査 |  |
| C146 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 高 | セキュリティ強化(挙動変更) | camel-undertow | camel-undertow - WebSocket endpoints apply the security provider, allowed roles and handlers | 該当コンポーネントを使用しているか grep。使用していれば、拒否される入力・必要な追加オプションがないか原文で確認し、結合テストで検証 | camel-undertow, ws://, wss://, UndertowSecurityProvider, securityConfiguration, securityProvider, allowedRoles, handlers, accessLog, 403 | WebSocket endpoints (`ws://` and `wss://`) now apply the `UndertowSecurityProvider` (the `securityConfiguration` or `securityProvider` option), the `allowedRoles` option, and the `handlers` and `accessLog` options, as HTTP endpoints do. Previously these options had no effect on WebSocket endpoints. They apply to the upgrade request: a client that the security provider rejects, or that connects to an endpoint with `allowedRoles` but no security provider, now gets the same status code as on an HTT | あり | WebSocket endpoints (`ws://` and `wss://`) now apply the `UndertowSecurityProvider` (the `securityConfiguration` or<br>`securityProvider` option), the `allowedRoles` option, and the `handlers` and `accessLog` options, as HTTP endpoints<br>do. Previously these options had no effect on WebSocket endpoints. They apply to the upgrade request: a client that<br>the security provider rejects, or that connects to an endpoint with `allowedRoles` but no security provider, now gets<br>the same status code as on an HTTP endpoint (for example `403`) and no connection is opened. The headers that the<br>security provider adds are set on the exchanges of the connection.<br><br>All the endpoints of a WebSocket path share its connections. The settings of the consumer apply to the path,<br>including the messages sent by producers on that path, and keep applying while the consumer is stopped; a path that<br>only has producers applies their settings. A connection that was opened before these settings applied does not<br>receive the messages of the producers, and it is closed when it sends a message.<br><br>A WebSocket client that connected without satisfying the configured security provider or allowed roles is now<br>rejected: it must authenticate, or the option must be removed from the WebSocket endpoint. | 未調査 |  |
| C147 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-undertow | camel-undertow - the producer refuses a request target or header value with control characters | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-undertow, 0xFF, IllegalArgumentException, CamelHttpPath, CamelHttpQuery | The undertow producer writes the path, query and header values of the outgoing request to the socket one character at a time, without validating them. A carriage return or line feed therefore broke the request into further lines, and a character above `0xFF` was written as a single, different byte (for example `U+010A` became a line feed). The producer now refuses to send a request path, query or header value that contains a control character or a character above `0xFF`, and fails the exchange w | あり | The undertow producer writes the path, query and header values of the outgoing request to the socket one character at<br>a time, without validating them. A carriage return or line feed therefore broke the request into further lines, and a<br>character above `0xFF` was written as a single, different byte (for example `U+010A` became a line feed). The producer<br>now refuses to send a request path, query or header value that contains a control character or a character above<br>`0xFF`, and fails the exchange with an `IllegalArgumentException` instead. A space and a tab, which do not change the<br>framing, are still allowed. Routes that put a percent-encoded control character (such as `%0D%0A`) into the<br>`CamelHttpPath` or `CamelHttpQuery` header, which the producer decodes before building the request, are the ones most<br>likely to be affected. | 未調査 |  |
| C148 | 4.18.5→4.18.6 | パッチ | 中 | 挙動変更 | camel-core | camel-core - pollEnrich: the body is null after a timeout also for a message that creates its body on demand | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 | camel-core, pollEnrich, poll, aggregationStrategy, null, variableReceive, GenericFileMessage, JmsMessage | When `pollEnrich` (or `poll`) times out and no `aggregationStrategy` is configured, the body is set to `null`, and with `variableReceive` the variable is `null`. For a message that creates its body on demand, the `null` did not stick: the next read created the body again. The message of a file consumer creates its body from its file, so after a timeout the body, or the variable of `variableReceive`, was the file of the incoming exchange instead of `null`. The messages of the JMS, sjms, mail, HTT | あり | When `pollEnrich` (or `poll`) times out and no `aggregationStrategy` is configured, the body is set to `null`, and with<br>`variableReceive` the variable is `null`. For a message that creates its body on demand, the `null` did not stick: the<br>next read created the body again. The message of a file consumer creates its body from its file, so after a timeout<br>the body, or the variable of `variableReceive`, was the file of the incoming exchange instead of `null`. The messages<br>of the JMS, sjms, mail, HTTP, XMPP, SMPP, SNMP and Spring WS consumers create their body on demand as well, so there it<br>was the payload of the consumer again.<br><br>The body, or the variable, is now `null` after a timeout for these consumers too. A route that read the incoming file<br>or payload from the body after a timed out `pollEnrich` must keep it before the `pollEnrich` instead, for example with<br>`variableReceive`, which leaves the body as it was. To keep the body empty, the message is replaced by a plain message<br>with the same message id, headers and attachments, so after a timeout it is no longer of the message type of the<br>consumer (such as `GenericFileMessage` or `JmsMessage`). | 未調査 |  |
| C149 | 4.18→4.19 | マイナー | 中 | 挙動変更 | (全般/その他) | Spring Boot | 該当コンポーネント/EIPの使用箇所を grep し、原文の挙動差分が自システムに影響するか確認 |  | This is our first release that supports Spring Boot v4. IMPORTANT: Spring Boot v3 is no longer supported. This also means Spring Framework v6 is also no longer supported. The Camel Spring component relies on Spring Framework v7 and what else Spring Boot v4 came with. |  | This is our first release that supports Spring Boot v4.<br><br>IMPORTANT: Spring Boot v3 is no longer supported.<br><br>This also means Spring Framework v6 is also no longer supported.<br>The Camel Spring component relies on Spring Framework v7 and what else Spring Boot v4 came with. | 未調査 |  |
